\begin{abstract}

Phototaxis, the process by which living organisms navigate toward optimal light conditions, is essential for motile photosynthetic microorganisms. Positive(negative) phototaxis denotes the motion directed towards(away from) the source of illumination. The main objective of this study is the numerical investigation of onset of bioconvection in a suspension of phototactic microorganisms illuminated by oblique collimated irradiation at the top. In this suspension, the algal cells absorb and anisotropically scatter incident light which influences the flow dynamics of the cells.

%The bio-thermal convection in a layer of porous medium saturated with a Newtonian fluid with phototactic microorganisms is studied on the basis of the Darcy-Brinkman model in this study. In this investigation, top boundary of the medium is irradiated by the collimated light and bottom boundary is subjected to heating or cooling. A linear analysis of the bio-thermal convection is carried out using a fourth order accurate finite difference scheme based on the Newton-Raphson-Kantorovich iterations for rigid-free and rigid-rigid boundary conditions. The influence of the Peclet number, Darcy number, critical light intensity and thermal Rayleigh number on bioconvective processes is analyzed and shown graphically. The results indicate that an increase in the Darcy number, critical light intensity stimulate the convection. Additionally, the thermal Rayleigh number delays the onset of the bioconvection.

%This study investigates the onset and stability of thermal bioconvection in a porous medium saturated with an algal suspension. The interplay between thermal effects, phototaxis, and porous structure is analyzed through linear stability theory. The governing equations are derived using the Navier-Stokes equations with the Boussinesq approximation, microorganism conservation equation, and energy equation. The analysis reveals that increasing the Darcy number, thermal Rayleigh number, and cell swimming speed significantly influences bioconvective patterns. The study provides new insights into optimizing biotechnological applications, such as biofuel production and industrial filtration.
\end{abstract}

