\begin{abstract}
% Humanoid robots require robust whole-body control to execute diverse tasks in human-centric environments. Existing methods for whole-body motion tracking often adopt teacher-student frameworks. However, they struggle to balance motion expressiveness and policy robustness, frequently resulting in averaged and unexpressive behaviors. In this paper, we propose Uni-Tracker, which addresses this challenge through a novel three-stage training paradigm. In the first stage, an oracle policy is trained without environmental domain randomization to fully exploit its capacity for accurately tracking diverse reference motions. In the second stage, this policy is distilled using a Conditional Variational Autoencoder (CVAE), preserving expressive motion representations under partial observation constraints. In the third stage, environmental domain randomization is introduced, and only the CVAE prior is fine-tuned, allowing the policy to adapt to real-world variations without compromising its expressive capabilities. Experimental results show that Uni-Tracker is the first universal whole-body motion tracker that enables a humanoid robot to robustly track a wide range of expressive motions using a single policy, effectively achieving a balance between expressiveness and robustness.

Humanoid robots must achieve diverse, robust, and generalizable whole-body control to operate effectively in complex, human-centric environments. However, existing methods, particularly those based on teacher-student frameworks often suffer from a loss of motion diversity during policy distillation and exhibit limited generalization to unseen behaviors. In this work, we present UniTracker, a simplified yet powerful framework that integrates a Conditional Variational Autoencoder (CVAE) into the student policy to explicitly model the latent diversity of human motion. By leveraging a learned CVAE prior, our method enables the student to retain expressive motion characteristics while improving robustness and adaptability under partial observations. The result is a single policy capable of tracking a wide spectrum of whole-body motions with high fidelity and stability. Comprehensive experiments in both simulation and real-world deployments demonstrate that UniTracker significantly outperforms MLP-based DAgger baselines in motion quality, generalization to unseen references, and deployment robustness, offering a practical and scalable solution for expressive humanoid control. 

% Whole-body motion tracking is a promising way for building general-purpose humanoid robots. However, achieving a universal motion tracker is challenging due to three critical requirements: expressiveness of motion execution, robustness to perturbations and generalization to unseen motions. Existing works often emphasize one or two of these aspects, failing to excel in all three simultaneously. In this work, we present UniTracker, a simplified yet powerful framework that achieves robustness and generalization under partial observations while retaining the expressiveness of motion execution to the maximum. Our method integrates a Conditional Variational Autoencoder (CVAE) into the teacher-student learning pipeline, resulting in a single policy that may track a wide range of whole-body motions with high fidelity and stability. Extensive experiments in both simulation and real world demonstrate that by combining motion imitation with latent-space regularization, our method achieves superb expressiveness of motion execution, robustness to perturbations and generalization to unseen motions, offering a practical and scalable solution for general whole-body control for humanoid robots. Videos and additional information can be found at \url{https://yinkangning0124.github.io/Humanoid-UniTracker/}.

\end{abstract}


