\textit{Symmetric and antisymmetric integrals---}The covariance between two state observables $x_n$ and $y_n$ is 
\begin{align}
\label{eq:corr-fun-1}
    C_{yx}^\tau \equiv \Delta\boldsymbol{y}^\intercal e^{\tau \mathbb{W}} \text{diag}(\boldsymbol{\pi}) \Delta\boldsymbol{x}\,,
\end{align}
where $\text{diag}(\boldsymbol{\pi})$ denotes the diagonal matrix with nonzero elements $(\pi_1,\dots,\pi_N)$. The time integral of \cref{eq:corr-fun-1} is
\begin{align}
\label{eq:corr-fun-2}
    \int_0^\infty C_{yx}^\tau \,d\tau 
     &=  \Delta\boldsymbol{y}^\intercal \int_0^\infty e^{\tau \mathbb{W}}\big[\mathbb{1}  -\boldsymbol{\pi}\boldsymbol{1}^\intercal \big]d\tau \,\text{diag}(\boldsymbol{\pi})\Delta\boldsymbol{x} \nonumber\\ 
     &=  - \Delta\boldsymbol{y}^\intercal \mathbb{W}^D\text{diag}(\boldsymbol{\pi})\Delta\boldsymbol{x} \,,
\end{align}
where we used $\boldsymbol{1}^\intercal\text{diag}(\boldsymbol{\pi})\Delta \boldsymbol{x} = \boldsymbol{\pi}^\intercal \Delta\boldsymbol{x} = 0$ in the first identity and \cref{eq:Drazin-def} for $\mathbb{W}^D$. As a result, the SICov, \cref{eq:covariance-def}, and AICov, \cref{eq:asymmetry-def}, can be written as
\begin{subequations}
\begin{align}
\label{eq:symmetry-C}
    \langle\langle y, x \rangle\rangle_+ &= \Delta\boldsymbol{y}^\intercal\mathbb{C}^+\Delta\boldsymbol{x} \,,\\
\label{eq:asymmetry-C}
    \langle\langle y, x \rangle\rangle_- &= \Delta\boldsymbol{y}^\intercal\mathbb{C}^-\Delta\boldsymbol{x} \,,  
\end{align}
\end{subequations}
with the unified matrices
\begin{align}
\label{eq:matZ-def}
    \mathbb{C}^\pm &\equiv -\big(\mathbb{W}^D\text{diag}(\boldsymbol{\pi}) \pm [\mathbb{W}^D\text{diag}(\boldsymbol{\pi})]^\intercal\big)\,,
\end{align}
which, using \cref{eq:Drazin-property} and $\mathbb{W}^D\text{diag}(\boldsymbol{\pi})\boldsymbol{1} = \mathbb{W}^D\boldsymbol{\pi} = 0$, can be rewritten as
\begin{align}
\label{eq:matZ-1}
    \mathbb{C}^\pm 
    &=-\mathbb{W}^D\big[\text{diag}(\boldsymbol{\pi})\mathbb{W}^\intercal \pm \mathbb{W}\text{diag}(\boldsymbol{\pi})\big](\mathbb{W}^D)^\intercal\,.
\end{align}

The activity matrix $\mathbb{A}$ has off-diagonal elements 
\begin{align}
\label{eq:activity}
    A_{kl} \equiv W_{kl}\pi_l + W_{lk}\pi_k\,,
\end{align}
and diagonal elements $A_{ll} \equiv -\sum_{k\neq l}A_{kl}$, where $A_{kl}$ is the activity, also known as traffic or frenesy \cite{maes2020frenesy}. 
From \cref{eq:matZ-1}, we find
\begin{align}
\label{eq:matC-plus-result-1}
    \mathbb{C}^+ = - \mathbb{W}^D\mathbb{A}(\mathbb{W}^D)^\intercal\,.
\end{align}
This agrees with the symmetric covariance matrices derived in Refs.~\cite{ptaszynski2024frr, lapolla2020spectral, lapolla2019manifestations, lapolla2018unfolding}. 
The current matrix $\mathbb{J}$ has off-diagonal elements 
\begin{align}
\label{eq:current}
    J_{kl} \equiv W_{kl}\pi_l  - W_{lk}\pi_k\,,
\end{align}
and zero diagonal elements $J_{ll} \equiv 0$, where
$J_{kl}$ is the net probability current from state $l$ to state $k$. 
Using \cref{eq:matZ-1}, we find
\begin{align}
\label{eq:matC-minus-result-1}
    \mathbb{C}^- = \mathbb{W}^D \mathbb{J}(\mathbb{W}^D)^\intercal\,, 
\end{align}
which, to our knowledge, was not known before. 
The fact that the core properties of the SICov and AICov, \cref{eq:symmetry-C} and \cref{eq:asymmetry-C}, are encoded in the activity and current matrices, \cref{eq:matC-plus-result-1,eq:matC-minus-result-1}, is our first main result.

\textit{SICov and excess observables---}Our second main result will now be to derive exact relations for the SICov in terms of the statistics of the excess observables weighted by the activity. 
Using \cref{eq:matC-plus-result-1} in \cref{eq:symmetry-C}, we find
\begin{align}
    \label{eq:covariance-C}
    \langle\langle y, x \rangle\rangle_+ &=- \boldsymbol{Y}^\intercal\mathbb{A}\boldsymbol{X} = -\boldsymbol{X}^\intercal\mathbb{A}\boldsymbol{Y}\,,
\end{align}
where, following \cref{eq:X-Drazin}, $\boldsymbol{X}^\intercal=- \Delta\boldsymbol{x}^\intercal \mathbb{W}^D$ and
$\boldsymbol{Y}=- (\mathbb{W}^D)^\intercal \Delta\boldsymbol{y}$. Rewriting $-\sum_{kl}X_{k}A_{kl}Y_l$ with $A_{ll} = -\sum_{k\neq l}A_{kl}$, and reducing the summation to $\sum_{k<l}$, we obtain
\begin{align}
\label{eq:FRR-excess}
\langle\langle y, x \rangle\rangle_+ &= \sum_{k<l}A_{kl}(X_{k} - X_{l})(Y_{k} - Y_{l})\,.
\end{align}
As a result, the variances of observables $\boldsymbol{x}$ and $\boldsymbol{y}$ read $\langle\langle x, x \rangle\rangle_+ = \sum_{k<l}A_{kl}(X_{k} - X_{l})^2$ and $\langle\langle y, y \rangle\rangle_+ =  \sum_{k<l}A_{kl}(Y_{k} - Y_{l})^2$. 
In \cref{sec:response}, we show how \cref{eq:FRR-excess} can be related to recently discovered fluctuation-response relations \cite{aslyamov2025frr, ptaszynski2024frr, ptaszynski2025frr-mixed}. Moreover, using \cite{ptaszynski2024frr,ptaszynski2025frr-mixed}, one can see how, close to equilibrium, \cref{eq:FRR-excess} reduces to the fluctuation-dissipation theorem. 

As detailed in \cref{sec:activity-bound}, \cref{eq:FRR-excess} can also be used to upper bound the SICov 
\begin{align}
\label{eq:Activity-bound}
    \langle\langle x, x \rangle\rangle_+ \leq ||x||^2 A T_\text{max}^2\,,
\end{align}
where $||x|| \equiv \min\{|\min_{n}\Delta x_n |, \max_{n}\Delta x_n\}$, $A \equiv \sum_{k<l}A_{kl}$ is the total activity, and $T_\text{max} \equiv \max_{\substack{k \neq l \\ A_{kl} \neq 0}}(T_{kl})$ is the longest expected time between directly connected states. This value identifies the worst-case local bottleneck for one-way movement in the network, indicating where the process is most sluggish in terms of direct connectivity.
The bound \eqref{eq:Activity-bound} differs from other known upper bounds on fluctuations \cite{bakewell2023general, ptaszynski2024frr}.


\textit{AICov and excess observables---}Our third main result will be to derive an exact expression for the AICov in terms of excess observables. 
Using \cref{eq:matC-minus-result-1,eq:X-Drazin}, we express the AICov in \cref{eq:asymmetry-C} as
\begin{align}
\label{eq:asymmetry-result-J}
    \langle\langle y, x \rangle\rangle_- &= \boldsymbol{Y}^\intercal \mathbb{J} \,\boldsymbol{X} = - \boldsymbol{X}^\intercal \mathbb{J} \,\boldsymbol{Y}\,.
\end{align}
In coordinate form, $\langle\langle y, x \rangle\rangle_- = \sum_{kl} Y_k X_l J_{kl}$ quantifies the AICov through weighted currents between states. 
At equilibrium, since $J_{kl}=0$ for $\forall k,l$, \cref{eq:asymmetry-result-J} implies Onsager reciprocity $\langle\langle y, x \rangle\rangle_- = 0$. 
Away from equilibrium, since $J_{kl} = - J_{lk}$, we obtain
\begin{align}
\label{eq:asymmetry-result-nonreciprocity}
    \langle\langle y, x \rangle\rangle_- = \sum_{k<l} (Y_k X_l - Y_l X_k) J_{kl} \,.
\end{align}
Doing so reveals that the AICov measures the sum over all pairs of connected states of the nonreciprocities in excess observables, $(Y_k X_l - Y_l X_k)$, weighted by the corresponding current, $J_{kl}$. 

The AICov in \cref{eq:asymmetry-result-nonreciprocity} can also be expressed in terms MFPTs using \cref{eq:X-MFTS}. Indeed, considering the asymmetry between one-point observables $\boldsymbol{x}=(x_n)$ and $\boldsymbol{y}=(y_m)$, we find that
\begin{align}
    C^-_{nm} &= \pi_n \pi_m \sum_{kl}T_{mk}T_{nl}J_{kl}\nonumber\\
    &=\pi_n \pi_m \sum_{k<l}(T_{mk}T_{nl}-T_{ml}T_{nk})J_{kl}\,,
\end{align}
thus revealing that the AICov is also a measure of non-reciprocity in MFPTs.

\textit{Geometric bounds---}We can now make use of the geometric approach to thermodynamic bounds developed in \cite{ohga2023thermodynamic} on our exact results \cref{eq:asymmetry-result-nonreciprocity,eq:FRR-excess}. We consider the ratio
\begin{align}
\label{eq:ratio-1}
    \frac{\langle\langle y, x \rangle\rangle_- }{\langle\langle x, x \rangle\rangle_+ + \langle\langle y, y \rangle\rangle_+} = \frac{2 \sum_{k<l} J_{kl} \Omega_{kl} }{\sum_{k<l}A_{kl} L_{lk}^2}\,,
\end{align}
where $L_{lk} \equiv \sqrt{( X_l -  X_k)^2+( Y_l -  Y_k)^2}$ and $\Omega_{kl} \equiv   \tfrac{1}{2}(X_{l} Y_{k} - X_{k}Y_{l})$. Using Eqs.~(3) and (S7) in \cite{ohga2023thermodynamic} for the right-hand side of \cref{eq:ratio-1}, we find
\begin{align}
\label{eq:geometric-bound}
    |\langle\langle y, x \rangle\rangle_-| \leq \frac{\langle\langle x, x \rangle\rangle_+ + \langle\langle y, y \rangle\rangle_+}{2}\max_c\frac{\tanh\tfrac{|\mathcal{F}_c|}{2n_c}}{\tanh\tfrac{\pi}{n_c}}\,,
\end{align}
where the maximum is taken over all topological (Schnakenberg \cite{schnakenberg1976network}) cycles of the graph associated with the Markov jump process, $\mathcal{F}_c$ is the affinity of the cycle (defined as the natural logarithm of the ratio between the product of the rates along the forward orientation of the cycle and the product of the rates along the backward orientation), and $n_c$ is the number of states in the cycle. 
We note that \cref{eq:geometric-bound} could be further improved using uniform cycles \cite{pietzonka2016affinity}, as discussed in \cite{ohga2023thermodynamic}.

\textit{Excess bounds---}In \cref{sec:excess-bound}, we derive a second inequality, termed the excess bound, which reads
\begin{align}
\label{eq:excess-bound}
    |\langle\langle y, x \rangle\rangle_-| &\leq \min \big\{\Delta Y\langle\langle x, x \rangle\rangle_+^{1/2} \,, \Delta X \langle\langle y, y \rangle\rangle_+^{1/2} \big\} \Pi^{1/2}\,,
\end{align}
where $\Pi \equiv \sum_{k<l}J_{kl}^2/A_{kl}$ is the pseudo-entropy production~\cite{dechant2021improving,shiraishi2021optimal,van2022unified,dechant2022minimum} and where 
\begin{align}
    \Delta X \equiv \max_n X_n - \min_n X_n\,, \quad \Delta Y \equiv \max_n Y_n - \min_n Y_n\,,
\end{align}
measure the heterogeneity of the excess observables. We note that \cref{eq:excess-bound} can also be expressed in terms of the steady-state entropy production $\dot{\sigma}=\sum_{k<l}J_{kl}\ln(W_{kl}/W_{lk})$ since $\Pi\leq \dot{\sigma}/2$. 
In the case of a fully connected network ($A_{kl}\neq 0$ for $\forall k, l$), using \cref{eq:response-6} with the static responses [\cref{sec:response}], we find
\begin{align}
\label{eq:DeltaX-response}
    \Delta X = \max_{k<l}\Bigg|\frac{1}{J_{kl}}\sum_{n}x_n\frac{d\pi_n}{dB_{kl}}\Bigg|=\max_{k<l}\Bigg|\frac{1}{J_{kl}}\frac{d\langle x \rangle}{dB_{kl}}\Bigg|\,.
\end{align}
For the second identity, we assumed that $x_n$ does not depend on the symmetric parameterization $B_{kl}=\sqrt{W_{kl}W_{lk}}$.

\textit{Simple applications---}We first consider the simple model for a molecular motor depicted in \cref{fig:fig-1}(a). All transitions are reversible and the one between states $1$ and $2$ is driven by chemical reservoirs generating a chemical potential difference $\Delta \mu$. 
\cref{fig:fig-1}(b) depicts the excess observables associated with the state observables $\boldsymbol{x} = (1,0,0)^\intercal$ and $\boldsymbol{y} = (0,1,0)^\intercal$ which are projectors on state $1$ and $2$, respectively. 
\cref{fig:fig-1}(c) depicts the AICov and its geometric and excess bound. 
At finite driving $\Delta \mu > 0$, the AICov measures the nonreciprocity of the cross-correlation between states $1$ and $2$. It vanishes at equilibrium ($\Delta \mu = 0$ and $\Pi = 0$) where Onsager reciprocity is restored, but it also vanishes for large driving in this model. We also note that the bell-shaped behavior of the AICov as a function of $\Delta\mu$ is captured by the excess bound but not by the geometric bound.
We next consider a network with a multicyclic topology and numerically verify the geometric and excess bounds in \cref{fig:fig-2}.
