\begin{abstract}
We present \textbf{TIP-Search}, a deadline-aware inference scheduling framework for real-time market prediction under uncertainty. In latency-critical environments such as high-frequency trading, inference decisions must be made within strict time budgets despite variable model performance and heterogeneous data distributions. TIP-Search dynamically selects one model per task from a pool of pre-trained deep learning models, balancing inference latency and predictive accuracy. Our approach leverages offline profiling and domain-aware generalization to route each task to the fastest eligible model expected to meet its deadline. Extensive evaluations on three real-world limit order book (LOB) datasets—FI-2010, Binance BTC/USDT, and LOBSTER AAPL—demonstrate that TIP-Search achieves up to 8.5\% higher accuracy than fixed or random strategies while satisfying 100\% of deadline constraints. We further conduct comprehensive ablation studies and analyze latency–throughput tradeoffs across scheduling policies.
\end{abstract}

\begin{IEEEkeywords}
Real-time inference, deadline scheduling, heterogeneous models, market prediction, accuracy–latency tradeoff, LOB datasets
\end{IEEEkeywords}

\section{Introduction}

Modern financial systems increasingly rely on deep learning models for tasks such as limit order book (LOB) prediction, algorithmic trading, and price movement forecasting. These applications often operate under stringent latency constraints, where decision quality is tightly coupled with response time. The accuracy–latency tradeoff therefore becomes a central consideration in real-time deployment.

Deep learning inference, however, is inherently variable in latency, especially when models differ in size, architecture, and domain specialization. For instance, a convolutional model trained on high-frequency data from Binance may offer low-latency predictions but perform poorly when evaluated on Nasdaq-based FI-2010 data. Conversely, larger or more robust models may generalize well across market regimes yet incur higher computational cost. In high-stakes scenarios where each millisecond can alter trading outcomes, the need to reconcile inference accuracy with temporal predictability becomes imperative.

Traditional approaches to model deployment in financial AI pipelines typically assume fixed model allocation, offline provisioning for worst-case latency, or simplified selection heuristics. These methods fail to adapt to real-time variability in both data characteristics and system performance. Market environments are non-stationary, exhibiting bursty task arrivals and shifting distributions. In such settings, static selection strategies often lead to suboptimal performance: either violating latency guarantees or compromising predictive accuracy due to model–data mismatch.

This work addresses the problem of online model selection under deadline constraints by introducing \textbf{TIP-Search}, a framework for \emph{Time-Predictable Inference Scheduling}. Rather than assigning a single model to all incoming tasks, TIP-Search dynamically selects a model per task from a heterogeneous pool, each trained on a distinct real-world financial dataset. The selection process is guided by offline latency profiling and estimated generalization performance, enabling per-task routing to the fastest eligible model with the highest expected accuracy.

TIP-Search is built on the insight that the inference-time–accuracy landscape forms a Pareto frontier. When sufficient slack is available, the scheduler favors more accurate models; under tighter deadlines, it prioritizes those with faster response time. Crucially, this tradeoff is navigated without violating task-level timing constraints.

