\caption{\raggedright TIP-Search architecture: Real-time LOB tasks are dispatched to a model pool based on deadline monitoring and selector logic.}
\label{fig:architecture}
\end{figure}

To evaluate the efficacy of TIP-Search, we conduct experiments using three publicly available financial datasets: FI-2010, Binance BTC/USDT, and LOBSTER AAPL. Each dataset is used to train one DeepLOB model, forming a heterogeneous ensemble. Using realistic task arrival rates and fixed deadlines, we show that TIP-Search consistently improves prediction accuracy over fixed-model and random-selection baselines while maintaining 100\% deadline satisfaction.

This paper makes the following contributions. First, we formulate the problem of deadline-constrained model selection in the context of real-time market prediction. Second, we propose TIP-Search, a practical and modular scheduling framework that combines model latency profiling with source-aware accuracy estimation. Third, we demonstrate through comprehensive evaluation that TIP-Search outperforms conventional heuristics across multiple datasets and decision conditions. Finally, we provide ablation analyses and design insights to support deployment in high-frequency inference environments.

To the best of our knowledge, TIP-Search represents the first attempt to jointly optimize for inference accuracy and real-time deadline satisfaction in the context of financial deep learning under uncertainty. The proposed framework contributes to a growing body of work on robust AI scheduling, and opens new directions for time-aware inference orchestration in latency-critical domains.


\section{Background and Motivation}

Real-time financial decision systems, such as high-frequency trading and limit order book (LOB) prediction engines, operate under stringent temporal constraints. In such settings, even minor delays in model inference can lead to missed market opportunities or suboptimal trades. Consequently, the deployment of deep learning models in these environments must address not only the predictive performance of each model but also its computational latency. The core operational requirement is the ability to deliver high-quality predictions within strict and deterministic timing bounds.

Despite significant advances in model design for financial time series prediction, the integration of these models into real-time systems remains challenging. Inference latency varies considerably across different architectures and deployment configurations due to differences in computational complexity, parameter size, and hardware contention. Additionally, task input characteristics—such as the volatility of market dynamics or the structural patterns of LOB events—can influence inference behavior, leading to heterogeneous response times even within the same model.

Crucially, financial inputs arrive in an inherently stochastic manner. Unlike domains with stable periodic input rates, market data streams are characterized by burstiness and temporal irregularity, resulting from shifts in trader behavior, economic announcements, or liquidity shocks. This temporal uncertainty amplifies the difficulty of static resource allocation or fixed pipeline designs. Furthermore, the assumption that a single model can generalize across all market conditions and asset classes is often violated in practice. For instance, a model trained on historical Nasdaq data may perform poorly when evaluated on cryptocurrency order books, not due to architectural flaws but due to domain shift.

Previous attempts to mitigate these issues have largely focused on two types of strategies. The first class adopts conservative worst-case latency provisioning, in which all tasks are routed through the fastest model to guarantee deadline compliance. While this approach simplifies scheduling, it typically sacrifices prediction quality, especially when faster models are less expressive or trained on less relevant data. The second class relies on offline model selection, where a fixed model is assigned to all tasks under the assumption of input homogeneity. Such approaches ignore runtime variability and fail to adapt to changes in input source or model availability.

This paper addresses these limitations by proposing a dynamic and adaptive framework—TIP-Search—that selects the optimal model for each task at runtime based on latency constraints and expected predictive accuracy. The motivation is rooted in the observation that different models offer distinct tradeoffs in the latency–accuracy space, and that effective real-time inference demands a scheduling policy capable of navigating this tradeoff dynamically. TIP-Search avoids both the pessimism of worst-case provisioning and the rigidity of static model assignment by using a lightweight decision mechanism to select the fastest eligible model that is likely to yield the most accurate result, given the task’s characteristics and timing budget.

In summary, the motivation for TIP-Search arises from the confluence of three key challenges in real-time market inference: variability in model latency, heterogeneity in model generalization, and uncertainty in input arrival patterns. These challenges necessitate a fundamentally different approach to model scheduling—one that explicitly accounts for both performance and timing constraints while remaining adaptable to runtime conditions. The TIP-Search framework is designed to meet this need, providing a practical and theoretically grounded solution for real-world financial inference systems.

\section{Problem Formulation}

\subsection*{Notation}

To support formal analysis, we define key symbols in Table~\ref{tab:notation}.

