\subsection*{Problem Setting}

We consider a real-time inference scenario in which a financial prediction engine receives a continuous stream of input tasks, each corresponding to a limit order book (LOB) snapshot. Each task $t_i$ arrives at time $a_i$ and must be processed before its deadline $d_i = a_i + \Delta$, where $\Delta$ is a fixed system-wide latency budget.

The system is equipped with a model pool $\mathcal{M} = \{M_1, M_2, \ldots, M_K\}$, where each $M_k$ is trained on different financial datasets or under varying architectures. Each model is associated with an offline-measured latency $L_k$ and an (unknown at runtime) input-conditional accuracy $A_k(x)$. To facilitate scheduling, $\hat{A}_k(t_i)$ denotes an estimated accuracy, obtained via domain heuristics or validation feedback.

A key challenge arises from the trade-off between inference speed and accuracy. Faster models may underperform on out-of-distribution inputs, while more accurate models may exceed per-task latency budgets. Additionally, task inputs do not include labels or metadata indicating which model is optimal, which compounds the uncertainty in selection.

\subsection*{Optimization Objective}

The objective is to maximize the total number of correct predictions across all input tasks while satisfying their timing constraints. Formally, the optimization problem is expressed as:
\begin{align}
    \max \quad & \sum_i \mathbb{I}[\text{correct}(t_i, M_k)] \\
    \text{s.t.} \quad & a_i + L_k \le d_i, \quad \forall i
\end{align}
where $\mathbb{I}[\cdot]$ denotes the indicator function returning 1 if the prediction made by $M_k$ for $t_i$ is correct.

This formulation assumes that latency values $L_k$ are deterministic and accurately profiled. However, the true accuracy $A_k(x)$ remains unknown at decision time, motivating the use of accuracy estimators $\hat{A}_k(t_i)$ derived from task features or past performance.

\subsection*{TIP-Search Policy Formulation}

TIP-Search approaches this problem by first filtering out models that violate the deadline constraint, and then selecting the one with the highest expected accuracy. This decision process is formalized as:
\begin{equation}
    M^\star(t_i) = \arg\max_{M_k \in \mathcal{F}(t_i)} \hat{A}_k(t_i),
\end{equation}
where $\mathcal{F}(t_i) = \{ M_k \in \mathcal{M} \mid a_i + L_k \le d_i \}$. By combining offline latency profiling with runtime accuracy estimation, TIP-Search enables per-task model selection under uncertainty, ensuring deadline compliance while pursuing optimal prediction quality.

\section{Theoretical Analysis}

This section provides a formal study of TIP-Search’s schedulability, computational complexity, and accuracy gap relative to an idealized oracle policy.

\subsection{Schedulability Guarantee}

We define a task $t_i$ as schedulable if there exists at least one model $M_k$ such that $L_k \le \Delta$. Assuming model latencies are fixed and known offline, we obtain the following result:

\begin{theorem}[Feasibility Guarantee]
If $\exists M_k \in \mathcal{M}$ such that $L_k \le \Delta$, then every task $t_i$ will be assigned to a model that satisfies its deadline $d_i = a_i + \Delta$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
TIP-Search constructs the eligible model set $\mathcal{E}(t_i) = \{ M_k \mid L_k \le \Delta \}$. Since this set is non-empty by assumption, the algorithm selects the model with highest estimated accuracy $\hat{A}_k(t_i)$ among the eligible models. Thus, the chosen model $M^*(t_i)$ satisfies $L_{M^*(t_i)} \le \Delta$, guaranteeing $a_i + L_{M^*(t_i)} \le d_i$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Runtime Complexity}

Let $K = |\mathcal{M}|$. TIP-Search performs:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
    \item $\mathcal{O}(K)$ operations to filter deadline-eligible models;
    \item $\mathcal{O}(K)$ operations to compute $\arg\max \hat{A}_k(t_i)$.
\end{itemize}
Hence, the total per-task runtime is $\mathcal{O}(K)$—constant for bounded-size model pools.

\subsection{Accuracy Gap to Oracle Policy}

Let $M^{\text{oracle}}(t_i)$ denote the optimal selector that uses the true (but unknown) accuracy $A_k(t_i)$:

\begin{definition}[Oracle Selection Policy]
\[
M^{\text{oracle}}(t_i) = \arg\max_{M_k \in \mathcal{E}(t_i)} A_k(t_i)
\]
\end{definition}

TIP-Search instead uses:
\[
M^*(t_i) = \arg\max_{M_k \in \mathcal{E}(t_i)} \hat{A}_k(t_i)
\]

Let cumulative regret over $n$ tasks be:
\[
\mathcal{R}_n = \sum_{i=1}^{n} \left( A_{M^{\text{oracle}}(t_i)}(t_i) - A_{M^*(t_i)}(t_i) \right)
\]

\begin{theorem}[Bounded Regret under Estimation Error]
If $\lvert \hat{A}_k(t_i) - A_k(t_i) \rvert \le \epsilon$ for all $k$ and $t_i$, then:
\[
\mathcal{R}_n \le n \cdot \epsilon
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
At each task $t_i$, TIP-Search may miss the optimal model by at most $\epsilon$ in accuracy. Thus, the total regret across $n$ tasks is bounded by $n \cdot \epsilon$.
\end{proof}

\section{TIP-Search Algorithm Design}
\label{sec:design}

TIP-Search is designed as a deadline-aware inference orchestration framework, aimed at ensuring predictable response under uncertainty. It operates by dynamically selecting a model from a heterogeneous pool such that the execution satisfies latency constraints while maximizing expected predictive accuracy.

\subsection{System Architecture}

The system processes real-time tasks, each corresponding to a market snapshot, and performs deadline-constrained inference using a multi-model dispatch mechanism. The architecture comprises three key components: (1) a deadline monitor that computes the residual slack for each task; (2) a model selector that filters and ranks eligible models according to latency constraints and estimated accuracy; and (3) a model pool consisting of multiple pre-trained models, each with distinct latency–accuracy tradeoffs.

Figure~\ref{fig:architecture} illustrates the TIP-Search workflow. When a new task arrives, its deadline is computed based on the arrival time and a fixed budget. The scheduler evaluates all available models for latency eligibility and selects the one with the highest expected accuracy among them.

\subsection{Latency Profiling and Accuracy Estimation}

Prior to deployment, all models are profiled to record their average latency under warm-up conditions. These latency profiles $L_k$ are stored for all models $M_k \in \mathcal{M}$ and referenced at runtime to enforce deadline compliance. For predictive quality, each model is also associated with an empirical accuracy estimator $\hat{A}_k(t_i)$, which may be derived from offline validation or adaptive online tracking.

\subsection{Scheduling Logic}

Given a task $t_i$ arriving at time $a_i$ and deadline $d_i$, the scheduler identifies the set $\mathcal{E}$ of models for which $a_i + L_k \le d_i$ holds. Among the eligible models, it selects $M^*(t_i)$ that maximizes $\hat{A}_k(t_i)$. The scheduling strategy ensures that all dispatched tasks respect timing constraints.

\begin{algorithm}[t]
\caption{TIP-Search: Deadline-Aware Scheduling Policy}
\label{alg:tipsearch}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require Task $t_i$ with arrival $a_i$ and deadline $d_i$
\State $\mathcal{F}(t_i) \gets \emptyset$
\For{each model $M_k \in \mathcal{M}$}
    \If{$a_i + L_k \le d_i$}
        \State $\mathcal{F}(t_i) \gets \mathcal{F}(t_i) \cup \{M_k\}$
    \EndIf
\EndFor
\If{$\mathcal{F}(t_i) \neq \emptyset$}
    \State $M^\star(t_i) \gets \arg\max_{M_k \in \mathcal{F}(t_i)} \hat{A}_k(t_i)$
    \State Dispatch $t_i$ to $M^\star(t_i)$
\Else
    \State Drop $t_i$ or invoke fallback
\EndIf
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\subsection{Execution Workflow}

At runtime, the inference loop continuously monitors incoming tasks. Upon each arrival, the system computes the remaining time to deadline and invokes the scheduling logic to determine the model to use. The selected model processes the task immediately. Since all latency profiles are preloaded and accuracy estimators are lightweight, the scheduling overhead is negligible.

\subsection{Integration and Extension}

The modularity of TIP-Search allows it to be integrated into existing inference engines. It is agnostic to model architecture and supports GPU/CPU inference interchangeably. For extensibility, the accuracy estimation component can incorporate confidence scores, domain embeddings, or self-supervised feedback, making it adaptable to future research in learning-based scheduling.

\subsection{Theoretical Analysis and Feasibility Considerations}

To analyze the schedulability of TIP-Search under hard real-time constraints, we define the notion of task feasibility with respect to the system’s model pool $\mathcal{M}$. Given a task $t_i$ with arrival time $a_i$ and deadline $d_i$, TIP-Search guarantees feasibility if there exists at least one model $M_k$ such that $a_i + L_k \le d_i$. This ensures that task-level schedulability is preserved provided the model latency profiles $\{L_k\}$ are known and stable.

In the worst case, the selection logic incurs $\mathcal{O}(K)$ computational overhead per task, where $K$ is the number of candidate models. This ensures constant-time decision latency for bounded $K$, which holds in typical real-time deployments with pre-enumerated inference configurations.

Moreover, we observe that the scheduling decision reduces to a constrained maximization over a pre-filtered set, which can be formulated as a special case of deadline-constrained utility maximization. While the exact optimality of the surrogate accuracy function $\hat{A}_k$ cannot be guaranteed in non-stationary settings, TIP-Search is provably deadline-feasible given static latency bounds.


\section{Formal System Guarantees}
\label{sec:guarantees}

In this section, we provide formal guarantees on the schedulability and responsiveness of TIP-Search under uncertain and bursty load conditions. We also analyze the interference robustness of TIP-Search when multiple models are co-located on shared inference hardware.

\subsection{Schedulability under Bursty Arrivals}

Let $\lambda_{\text{max}}$ denote the maximum arrival rate (tasks per second), and $\mathbf{L_{\min}} = \min_{k} L_k$ denote the minimum model latency. For a system with strict deadline $\Delta$ and $K$ pre-profiled models, TIP-Search ensures feasibility under the following condition:

\begin{theorem}[Schedulability under Peak Load]
If $\lambda_{\text{max}} \cdot \mathbf{L_{\min}} \le 1$, then TIP-Search guarantees 100\% deadline satisfaction under bursty arrivals.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Since TIP-Search always selects the fastest eligible model $M_k$ satisfying $L_k \le \Delta$, the system can serve up to $1/L_k$ tasks per second per model. Under worst-case load, if $\lambda_{\text{max}} \le 1/L_{\min}$, at least one model remains schedulable at all times.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Response-Time Upper Bound}

Let $R_i$ denote the end-to-end response time for task $t_i$ under TIP-Search scheduling. Then:

\begin{theorem}[Response Time Bound]
Assuming zero queuing delay and deterministic latency $L_k$ per model, we have:
\[
R_i \le \min_{M_k \in \mathcal{F}(t_i)} L_k
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By construction, TIP-Search filters models satisfying $a_i + L_k \le d_i$ and chooses the one with highest $\hat{A}_k(t_i)$. Thus, $L_k \le \Delta$ and $R_i \le L_k$ for the selected model.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Robustness to Co-located Interference}

In multi-model deployments, concurrent tasks may share inference hardware, leading to performance interference. TIP-Search is robust to such contention due to its latency-awareness. We formalize this as:

\begin{definition}[Contention-Aware Schedulability]
Let $\tilde{L}_k = L_k + \delta_k$ be the observed latency under co-located interference. If $\exists M_k$ such that $\tilde{L}_k \le \Delta$, TIP-Search preserves deadline feasibility.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Interference-Robust Scheduling]
If the interference margin $\delta_k$ satisfies $\max_k \delta_k \le \epsilon$ and $\min_k L_k + \epsilon \le \Delta$, then TIP-Search remains deadline-feasible.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Follows directly from the eligibility check in the TIP-Search selection policy, which can be extended to use $\tilde{L}_k$ at runtime.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Robustness under DVFS and Energy Constraints}

Modern embedded systems often use dynamic voltage and frequency scaling (DVFS) to reduce power consumption. Let $L_k(f)$ denote the latency of model $M_k$ at frequency $f$. Under scaling, TIP-Search remains feasible if:

\begin{theorem}[DVFS Compatibility]
If there exists frequency $f$ such that $\min_k L_k(f) \le \Delta$, then TIP-Search preserves feasibility under DVFS.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Offline profiling must include $L_k(f)$ at various $f$. During runtime, TIP-Search can select the optimal $(M_k, f)$ pair such that $L_k(f) \le \Delta$, while maximizing $\hat{A}_k(t_i)$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Resource Isolation in Multi-GPU Scheduling}

In multi-GPU systems, different models may be bound to disjoint GPU cores. Let $G_j$ denote GPU $j$, and $\mathcal{M}_j$ the model set resident on $G_j$. Then:

\begin{theorem}[GPU Isolation Guarantee]
If each GPU $G_j$ hosts models with $L_k \le \Delta$, and task routing ensures $t_i \mapsto G_j$ iff $M_k \in \mathcal{M}_j$ is feasible, TIP-Search guarantees per-GPU deadline compliance.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Per-GPU feasibility follows from intra-GPU eligibility checks and independence of scheduling across $G_j$.
\end{proof}


\section{Experimental Evaluation}
\label{sec:experiments}

This section presents an empirical evaluation of TIP-Search under deadline-constrained market prediction workloads. The experiments are designed to assess the framework’s ability to maintain real-time responsiveness while maximizing predictive accuracy, as well as to quantify its generalization capacity, scheduling efficiency, and comparative performance against representative baselines.

\subsection{Experimental Setup and Datasets}

To simulate realistic online inference scenarios, we construct a heterogeneous workload using three distinct financial limit order book (LOB) datasets. The FI-2010 dataset~\cite{b3} comprises ten days of Nasdaq LOB data annotated with five-class mid-price movements. The Binance BTC/USDT dataset contains a 2025/05/10 snapshot of raw cryptocurrency trade events labeled for three-class price direction. The LOBSTER AAPL dataset provides high-frequency order book data for Apple Inc. over ten trading days, representing a more stable equity market.

Each dataset is used to independently train a DeepLOB model~\cite{b4}, yielding three heterogeneous models in the inference pool. These models are deployed on a single GPU, and runtime inference is triggered every 5ms, with a strict 10ms deadline per task. Each task includes an LOB snapshot, a ground-truth directional label, and an identifier of the dataset source. The TIP-Search framework must select a model per task to maximize predictive performance without violating the deadline constraint.

\subsection{Evaluation Protocol and Metrics}

We evaluate all scheduling policies on a shared stream of 298 tasks, sampled evenly across the three datasets. Each policy operates in isolation under identical inter-arrival and hardware conditions. Four primary metrics are collected: prediction accuracy under deadline constraint, deadline satisfaction rate, average inference latency per model, and total task throughput. These jointly reflect both effectiveness and efficiency of the tested strategies.

\subsection{Generalization Performance Across Domains}

The generalization behavior of individual models is examined by constructing an accuracy matrix where each row corresponds to the input source domain and each column to a deployed model. The results, visualized in Figure~\ref{fig:table_ii}, reveal significant asymmetries. The Binance and LOBSTER models exhibit strong cross-domain accuracy, while the FI-2010 model demonstrates poor generalization when applied to unseen market distributions. These discrepancies underscore the importance of dynamic model selection in heterogeneous environments.

