\subsection{Strategy-Level Comparative Analysis}

To quantify the benefit of adaptive scheduling, we compare TIP-Search against three baseline policies: a fixed-model strategy that always dispatches to one model, a random selection strategy among deadline-eligible models, and a source-aware strategy that assumes oracle access to ground-truth dataset labels. Figure~\ref{fig:ablation_strategy} presents the overall accuracy of each strategy across the three domains.

\subsection{Comparison with Classical Real-Time Scheduling}

To establish a connection with classical real-time scheduling theory, we simulate two canonical policies: Earliest Deadline First (EDF) and Least Laxity First (LLF). Although these strategies are traditionally used for CPU-level task dispatching rather than inference model selection, they provide valuable baselines for interpreting system responsiveness under dynamic load.

In our adaptation, EDF selects the model with the shortest remaining slack (i.e., $d_i - a_i - L_k$), while LLF uses the laxity ratio to rank candidate models. Both strategies are evaluated on the same 298-task stream. The resulting accuracy under deadline constraints was inferior to TIP-Search, suggesting that incorporating domain-specific accuracy estimation provides meaningful gains over purely temporal heuristics.

