\subsection{Summary and Observations}

The experimental results validate TIP-Search as a robust framework for deadline-aware scheduling under uncertainty. It achieves high prediction accuracy without deadline violations, generalizes across domains in the absence of source labels, and introduces minimal scheduling latency. These characteristics make it a compelling candidate for real-time inference in finance, robotics, and other high-frequency decision systems.

\section{Ablation Study and Discussion}
\label{sec:ablation}

To assess the effectiveness of TIP-Search in real-time inference, we conduct ablation studies that compare its performance with simplified decision strategies. Each strategy imposes a constraint on the model selection logic, thereby isolating the impact of specific scheduling principles.

\subsection{Experimental Conditions}

The ablation experiments are structured to examine four distinct policies under identical workload conditions. The \textit{Fixed} strategy consistently employs a single preselected model for all tasks, regardless of input characteristics. The \textit{Random} strategy selects among eligible models with uniform probability, without consideration of model quality. The \textit{Source-Only} strategy dispatches each task to the model trained on the same source as the input data. Finally, \textit{TIP-Search}—our proposed framework—dynamically selects the model with the highest expected accuracy from the subset of models that satisfy the deadline constraint.

Each policy is evaluated on a unified stream of market tasks drawn from multiple domains. Every task must be executed within a 10ms timing budget, and all experiments are executed under identical hardware and scheduling conditions to ensure comparability.

