\section{Deployment Considerations and Limitations}
\label{sec:deployment}

\subsection{Real-Time System Integration}

TIP-Search is architected to operate with minimal runtime overhead, making it amenable to integration with existing real-time financial inference pipelines. The scheduling mechanism executes entirely on the CPU, thereby avoiding contention with GPU-based inference workloads and preserving parallelism. In deployment environments with multiple resident models, TIP-Search supports concurrent GPU model sharing by leveraging preloaded weights in memory, which significantly reduces switching and loading latencies. Furthermore, the system assumes a warm-start initialization for all models, ensuring that cold-start latency penalties are eliminated prior to runtime. In dynamic market conditions, TIP-Search remains deadline-aware and supports per-task timing adaptation, enabling seamless operation under varying load intensities or application-specific latency constraints.

\subsection{Scalability in Heterogeneous Environments}

The proposed scheduling framework exhibits favorable scalability characteristics when managing a moderate number of candidate models, typically ranging from ten to twenty. This scale suffices for most real-time inference scenarios in market prediction, where models are often trained on distinct market instruments, time frames, or architectural variants. Nevertheless, scaling beyond this range may introduce computational overhead during eligibility filtering and accuracy ranking. In such cases, performance can be preserved through hierarchical scheduling mechanisms, lightweight surrogate scoring models, or pruning strategies that narrow the candidate set based on early-exit conditions or hardware-specific latency predictors.

\subsection{Limitations and Future Directions}

Despite its effectiveness in balancing inference quality and latency, TIP-Search is subject to several limitations. First, the system relies on the availability of multiple pre-trained models, each tailored to a specific domain or data distribution. This requirement introduces additional training and maintenance cost, particularly in evolving market environments. Second, TIP-Search currently assumes stationarity in model generalization profiles over time; however, financial markets are inherently non-stationary. Without mechanisms for continual learning or model retraining, TIP-Search may experience degradation in performance due to regime shifts or concept drift. Finally, the system estimates model accuracy using static or heuristically updated metrics, which may be insufficient under adversarial conditions or in the presence of previously unseen input distributions.

To address these challenges, future extensions of TIP-Search could incorporate online adaptation mechanisms, including self-supervised performance feedback, domain shift detection, or ensemble uncertainty quantification. Additionally, integrating low-cost latency prediction models or confidence-aware inference strategies may further enhance the system’s responsiveness in highly dynamic environments.

\subsection{Transferring to Embedded Inference Scenarios}

Although this work focuses on financial inference systems, the TIP-Search framework is well-suited for deployment in embedded real-time environments. In embedded AI systems, such as autonomous drones, mobile robots, and sensor-driven industrial controllers, real-time constraints and heterogeneous inference paths are common challenges.

TIP-Search's model-pool-based scheduling policy generalizes to scenarios where different model configurations correspond to distinct memory footprints, energy consumption profiles, or data preprocessing pipelines. For example, a robotic system may maintain one small CNN for low-latency edge detection and another transformer-based model for more accurate, but slower, spatial reasoning. TIP-Search can dynamically choose the inference path based on a task's urgency and timing budget.

Moreover, the offline latency profiling strategy aligns with the static analysis common in embedded systems, while the lightweight runtime dispatch ensures minimal CPU overhead. Because embedded systems often operate under constrained computational budgets, TIP-Search’s $\mathcal{O}(K)$ decision complexity and deadline-satisfaction guarantee offer practical benefits.

In future extensions, TIP-Search could integrate with existing real-time operating systems (RTOS) via inference-aware task schedulers, or be combined with early-exit networks to support dynamic voltage and frequency scaling (DVFS) in power-sensitive deployments.

\section{Relevance to Real-Time Systems Research}

Although TIP-Search is evaluated in the context of financial market prediction, the methodology and scheduling challenges it addresses are fundamental to the broader class of real-time AI systems. The proposed framework deals with online task arrival, strict latency constraints, heterogeneous execution paths, and dynamic model selection—elements commonly encountered in robotics, autonomous vehicles, embedded perception, and other cyber-physical applications.

In particular, the formulation of latency-aware, accuracy-guided model dispatch closely mirrors decision-making under bounded computational budgets in real-time embedded systems. Moreover, TIP-Search aligns with the traditional goals of real-time scheduling: deadline satisfaction, throughput maximization, and resource-aware dispatch.

By framing the deep learning inference pipeline as a real-time scheduling problem with accuracy–latency trade-offs, this work extends the RTSS discourse into the AI-inference domain. It demonstrates that rigorous real-time techniques are not only applicable but also beneficial in managing uncertainty and dynamics in data-driven settings. We believe TIP-Search opens new directions for integrating RT scheduling theory with adaptive model-based decision systems.


\section{Conclusion}

We proposed TIP-Search, a time-predictable inference scheduling framework that adaptively selects deep models for real-time financial market prediction. TIP-Search enables robust accuracy under deadline constraints by leveraging domain-aware generalization and latency profiling.

Through experiments on three real-world datasets (Binance, FI-2010, LOBSTER), we showed that TIP-Search outperforms fixed, random, and source-specific strategies. Our ablation studies confirm its advantage in handling model diversity and latency variation.

TIP-Search provides a practical and modular enhancement to real-time AI systems, especially in latency-sensitive domains such as trading, robotics, and cyber-physical systems.

