\vspace{-2mm}
\begin{abstract}
 	In this paper, we introduce a model-based deep-learning approach to solve finite-horizon continuous-time 
 stochastic control problems with jumps. We iteratively  train two neural networks: one to represent the optimal policy and the other to approximate the value function. Leveraging a continuous-time version of the dynamic programming principle, we derive two different training objectives based on the Hamilton--Jacobi--Bellman equation, ensuring that the networks capture the underlying stochastic dynamics. Empirical evaluations on different problems illustrate the accuracy and scalability of our approach, demonstrating its effectiveness in solving complex, high-dimensional stochastic control tasks.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction} 
\label{intro}
A  large class of dynamic decision-making problems under uncertainty can be  modeled as continuous-time  stochastic control problems. In this paper, we study a neural network-based numerical algorithm for such problems in high dimensions with finite time horizon and jumps. More precisely, we consider control problems of the form 
 \begin{equation} {\displaystyle 
	\sup_{\alpha} \E \edg{\int_0^T f(t, X^\alpha_t, \alpha_t) dt + F(X^\alpha_T)} , \label{pr} }
\end{equation}
for a finite horizon $T > 0$, where the supremum is over predictable control 
processes $\alpha = (\alpha_t)_{0 \le t \le T}$ taking  values in a subset $A \subseteq \R^m$.
The controlled process $X^\alpha$ evolves in a subset $D \subseteq \R^d$ according to
 \begin{align}
\hspace{-1mm}	dX^\alpha_t &= \beta(t, X^\alpha_t, \alpha_t) dt + \sigma(t, X^\alpha_t, \alpha_t) dW_t  + \int_E \hspace{-1mm} \gamma(t, X^\alpha_{t-}, z, \alpha_t) N^{\alpha}(dz, dt), \ \  X_0 = x \in D, \hspace{-1mm}\label{sde}
\end{align}for an initial condition $x \in D$, a $k$-dimensional Brownian motion $W$ and
a controlled random measure $N^{\alpha}$ on $E \times \R_+$ for $E = \R^l \setminus \{0\}$, 
and suitable functions $\beta \colon [0,T] \times D \times A \to \R^d$, 
$\sigma \colon [0,T] \times D \times A \to \R^{d \times k}$,
$\gamma \colon [0,T] \times D \times E \times A \to \R^d$. 
The functions $f \colon [0,T] \times D \times A \to \R$ 
and $F \colon D \to \R$ model the running and final rewards, respectively. We assume the controlled random measure is given by $N^{\alpha}(B \times [0,t]) = \sum_{j =1}^{M_t} 1_{\crl{Z_j \in B}}$, 
for measurable subsets $ B \subseteq E$, where $M$ is a Poisson process with an intensity of the form
$\lambda(t, X^\alpha_{t-}, \alpha_t)$ and $Z_1, Z_2, \dots$ are i.i.d.\ $E$-valued random vectors with  distribution $\mathcal{Z}$
such that, conditionally on $\alpha$, the random elements $W, M$ and $Z_1, Z_2, \dots$ are independent.  Our goal is to find an optimal  control denoted $\alpha^*$ and the corresponding value of the problem 
\eqref{pr}. In view of the Markovian nature of the dynamics \eqref{sde}, we work with feedback controls 
of the form $\alpha_t = \alpha(t, X^\alpha_{t-})$ for measurable functions $\alpha \colon [0,T) \times D \to A$
and introduce the value function $V: [0,T] \times D \to \mathbb{R}$ given by
\begin{equation}
	V(t,x)  =  \sup_{\alpha} \E \edg{\int_t^T f(s, X^\alpha_s, \alpha_s) ds + F(X^\alpha_T)  \hspace{0.2mm} \Big|  \hspace{0.2 mm}X^\alpha_t = x} .
	\label{opt_cont} 
\end{equation} Under suitable assumptions\footnote{see \textit{e.g.}\ Chapter 2 of \cite{bouchard2007introduction} and Chapter 5 of \cite{oksendal2019stochastic}.}, $V$ satisfies the following Hamilton--Jacobi--Bellman (HJB) equation: %for all $(t,x) \in [0,T) \times D$,
\begin{equation}
	\partial_t V(t, x) +\sup_{a \in A} H(t, x, V, a)=0, \quad V(T,x) = F(x) \, .
	\label{HJB2} 
\end{equation}
for the Hamiltonian $H : [0,T) \times D \times \mathcal{V}  \times A \rightarrow \mathbb{R}$ given\footnote{By $\mathcal{V}$ we denote the set of all functions in $C^{1,2}([0,T) \times \R^d)$
	such that the expectation $\mathbb{E}^{\mathcal{Z}}$ in \eqref{HJBB} is finite for all $t,x$ and $a$.}  by   \begin{equation}  \label{HJBB}
	\begin{aligned}
	H(t,x,V, a) &:=   f(t,x,a)   + \beta^T(t,x,a) \nabla_xV(t,x) + \frac{1}{2} {\rm Tr} 
	\edg{\sigma \sigma^T(t,x,a) \nabla^2_x V(t,x)}
	\\
	&  \hspace{22mm}+ \lambda(t,x,a) \, \E^{\mathcal{Z}} \edg{V (t, x + \gamma(t,x,Z_1,a)) - V(t,x)} \, ,
	\end{aligned}
\end{equation} where $\nabla_xV, \nabla^2_xV$ denote the gradient and Hessian of $V$  with respect to $x$.  Our approach consists in iteratively training two neural networks (NNs)  to approximate 
the value function $V$ and an optimal control denoted $\alpha^*$ attaining $V$. It has the following features\vspace{-1mm}:
\begin{itemize}
	\item It yields  accurate results for high-dimensional finite-horizon continuous-time stochastic controls in cases where the dynamics of the underlying stochastic processes are known.  \vspace{-0.5mm}
	\item It can effectively handle a combination of diffusive noise and random jumps with controlled  intensities. \vspace{-0.5mm}
	\item	It can handle general situations where the optimal control is not available in closed form but has to be learned together with the value function.  \vspace{-0.5mm}
	\item It approximates both the value function and optimal control at any  point $(t,x) \in [0,T) \times D$\vspace{-1mm}. 
\end{itemize}
While methods like finite differences, finite elements and spectral methods work well for partial integro-differential equations (PIDEs) in low dimensions, they suffer from the curse of dimensionality, and as a consequence, become infeasible in high dimensions. %Deep learning-based approaches can mitigate this issue by leveraging function approximation techniques that scale more efficiently with dimensions. 
 Recently, different deep learning based approaches for solving high-dimensional PIDEs have been proposed \citep{han2017deep,han2018solving, sirignano2018dgm, beck2021deep, blechschmidt2021three, lu2021deepxde, bruna2024neural}. They can directly be used to solve continuous-time stochastic control problems that admit an explicit solution for the optimal control in terms of the value function since in this case, the expression for the optimal control can be plugged into the HJB equation \eqref{HJB2}, which then reduces to a parabolic PIDE. One of the most promising numerical schemes addressing this challenge for PDEs--in the absence of jumps in \eqref{sde}--is based on Physics-Informed Neural Networks (PINNs), as introduced in \citet{raissi2017physics, raissi2019physics}. PINNs use the physical properties of the PDE itself to guide the training process instead of relying purely on data, and are able to provide a global solution of the PDE across all time and space  in the domain, which is well-suited for real-time applications  \citep{berg2018unified, mowlavi2023optimal, hainaut2024valuation}. %This contrasts with a second set of methods based on the Feymann-Kac formula for solving (nonlinear) parabolic second-order PDEs \citep{han2017deep, han2018solving, berner2020numerically,beck2021solving}, which tend to only provide local solutions and hence focus on computing the value of the PDE solution on a time-grid (at pre-specified times).
 On the other hand, if the optimal control is not available in closed form, we cannot plug it into the HJB equation, but instead, have to numerically approximate it while at the same time solving a parabolic PIDE.  Such implicit optimal control problems can no longer be solved directly with one of the deep learning methods mentioned above but require a specifically designed iterative approximation procedure. For low-dimensional problems with non-explicit optimal controls, a standard approach from the reinforcement learning (RL) literature is to use generalized policy iteration (GPI), a class of iterative algorithms that simultaneously approximate the value function and optimal control  \citep{jacka2017policy, sutton2018reinforcement}. Particularly popular are actor-critic methods going back to \citet{werbos1992approximate}, which have a separate memory structure to  represent the optimal control independently of the value function.  However, classical GPI schemes become impractical in high dimensions as the PIDE and optimization problem both have to be discretized and solved for every point in a finite grid.  This raises the need of meshfree methods for  solving implicit continuous-time stochastic control problems  in high dimensions with jumps and  continuous action space $A$. Several local approaches based on a time-discretization of \eqref{sde} have been explored in \citet{han2016deep, nusken2021solving,hure2021deep, bachouch2022deep, ji2022solving, li2024neural, domingo2024adjoint, domingo2024stochastic}. Alternatively, this can also be achieved using popular (deep) RL techniques including policy gradient methods such as A2C/A3C \citep{mnih2016asynchronous}, PPO \citep{schulman2017proximal} and TRPO  \citep{duan2016benchmarking};  Q-learning type algorithms with DQN \citep{mnih2013playing}, C51 \citep{bellemare2017distributional}, see also \cite{wang2020reinforcement,jia2023q, gao2024reinforcement}; and hybrid approaches  with DDPG \citep{lillicrap2015continuous} or SAC \citep{haarnoja2018soft}.   However, these model-free deep RL algorithms do not explicitly leverage  the known underlying dynamics \eqref{sde} of the  stochastic control problem  \eqref{pr} since they rely solely on sampling from the environment, using some time-discretization as well.\ As a result, they are less accurate in cases where the state dynamics \eqref{sde} are fully known and given. 
\\ \\
In this paper, we propose a deep model-based  approach which explicitly uses the given state dynamics \eqref{sde} by leveraging the continuous-time version  \eqref{HJB2} of the HJB equation. This removes  the need to simulate these underlying  jump-diffusion equations and, as a result, avoids discretization errors. Our approach combines GPI with PINNs, and extends \cite{doya2000reinforcement} as well as \cite{duarte2024machine}. It consists in approximating the value function and the optimal control in an actor-critic fashion with two separate NNs trained on sampled data from the space-time domain $[0,T) \times D$. It has the advantage that it provides a global approximation of the value function and optimal control available for all space-time points that can be evaluated online fast. We develop two related algorithms. The first one, GPI-PINN 1, approximates the value function by training a NN so that it minimizes the residuals of the HJB equation \eqref{HJB2}. While it works well in high dimensions for control problems without jumps, it becomes inefficient when jumps terms are included in  the dynamics \eqref{sde}. Our second algorithm, GPI-PINN 2,  circumvents the computation of the gradient $\nabla_x V$ and  Hessian $\nabla_x^2 V$, as well as the expectation with respect to the jump measure in \eqref{HJBB}, which makes it highly efficient for high-dimensional problems with jumps, even if the control is not available in closed form. We illustrate the efficacy and accuracy of our approach in different numerical examples and provide comparisons with popular RL and deep-learning control methods. Proofs of theoretical results and additional numerical experiments are given in the Appendix. \vspace{-1mm}
%Therefore, the numerical scheme proposed in this paper aims at solving CT stochastic optimal control in high dimensions with jumps, even when the control cannot be expressed explicitly, and  is inspired by both the PINN and the  RL theory, in the spirit of \citet{doya2000reinforcement}, \cite{duarte2024machine}. More precisely, this so-called GPI-PINN algorithm is obtained by combining PINNs on the HJB residuals together with an Actor-Critic structure built upon the GPI algorithm. This iterative GPI-PINN scheme approximates both the value function and the high-dimensional optimal control using two separate NNs, which are trained  on sampled data from the space-time domain. This provides a global approximation of the control and value function available across all time and space, which can then be evaluated online  fast. However, we will show that this first HJB-residual-based GPI-PINN algorithm  cannot efficiently handle jumps in the state dynamics. To address this, we introduce an enhanced GPI-PINN scheme 2 that leverages a recursive property of the value function, and that converges much faster to the optimal solution, especially in the presence of jumps with controlled intensities. %The contributions of this article are therefore threefold. We first introduce a new successive deep-learning approximation algorithm designed to solve high-dimensional CT stochastic optimal control problems of the form \eqref{pr}, even when the optimal control does not admit an explicit solution. We then propose a second, enhanced version of this GPI-PINN algorithm that can more efficiently  accommodate jumps in the state dynamics. 
