	However, evaluating the Hamiltonian based on a NN approximation $V_\theta$ of $V$ requires computing the gradients  $\nabla_x V_\theta(t,x)$ and Hessians $\nabla^2_x V_\theta(t,x)$ for each $(t,x) \in [0,T) \times D$, which becomes computationally expensive when the number of state variables $d$ increases. The next proposition, adapted from \citet{duarte2024machine}, uses a trick to bypass these costly computations.
\begin{proposition}\label{Prop1}
	Consider a function $v \in \mathcal{V}$ and, for given $(t,x) \in [0,T) \times D$, define the function $\psi: \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ \vspace{-2mm}by
	\begin{equation}
			\psi(h) := \sum_{i=1}^{k} v \hspace{-0.3mm}\left(t + \frac{h^2}{2k}, x+\frac{h}{\sqrt{2}}\sigma_i(t,x,a)+\frac{h^2}{2k}\beta(t,x,a)\right) ,
	\end{equation} where $\sigma_i(t,x,a)$ is the $i^{th}$ column of the $d\times k$ matrix $\sigma(t,x,a)$. Then,
	\begin{align}
		\psi''(0) =  \partial_t v(t,x) &+ 	\beta^\top\hspace{-0.5mm}(t, x, a)  \, \nabla_xv(t,x) + \frac{1}{2} \normalfont \text{Tr} \left[ \sigma \sigma^\top\hspace{-0.5mm}(t,x,a) \nabla^2_x v(t,x) \right] . 
		\label{eqprop}
	\end{align} 
\end{proposition} 
Proposition \ref{Prop1} offers two key insights. First, \eqref{eqprop} makes it possible to avoid  the computation of  the gradient and Hessian of $v$  by instead evaluating the second derivative of the univariate function $\psi$. Secondly, since the cost of evaluating the second derivative of $\psi$ using automatic differentiation is  a small multiple of the cost of computing  $ \psi(0)=v(t,x)$, the overall computational cost of evaluating the right-hand side of \eqref{eqprop} only scales as a small multiple of $k \cdot cost(v)$. Let us introduce the extended Hamiltonian
\begin{equation} \label{extHam}
	\mathcal{H}(t,x,v,a) := \partial_t v(t,x) + H(t,x,v,a),
\end{equation}
which by Proposition \ref{Prop1}, can be written as 
\begin{equation*}
	\mathcal{H}(t,x,v,a) =  \psi''(0) + f(t,x,a) + \lambda(t,x,a) \mathbb{E}^{\mathcal{Z}} \big[  v(t,x+\gamma(t,x,Z_1,a)) - v(t,x) \big] .
\end{equation*}	
Note that the right-hand side still contains the jump expectation $\mathbb{E}^{\mathcal{Z}}[v(t,x+\gamma(t,x,Z_1,a))]$, which requires numerical integration for each $(t,x) \in [0,T) \times D$ and which is then computationally costly to evaluate.  To bypass these numerical integrations, we introduce an expectation-free Hamiltonian $G:[0,T] \times D \times E \times \mathcal{V} \times A \to \mathbb{R}$ defined by
\begin{equation} \label{Ht} 
	\begin{aligned}
	 	G(t,x, z, v, a) := v(t,x)+& \partial_t v(t,x) + f(t,x,a)    +	\beta^\top\hspace{-0.5mm} (t, x, a) \, \nabla_x v(t, x) \\
	 &  \hspace{-28mm}+  \frac{1}{2} \normalfont \text{Tr} \left[ \sigma \sigma^\top\hspace{-0.5mm}(t,x,a)\nabla^2_x v(t,x) \right]   + \lambda(t,x,a)  \left[ v(t,x+\gamma(t,x,z,a)) - v(t,x) \right] .  
	\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
Moreover, for a given Markov control $\alpha \colon [0,T) \times D \to A$ with resulting 
state dynamics $X^{\alpha}$, we denote by $V^{\alpha} \colon [0,T] \times D \to \R$ the corresponding 
reward functional
\[
V^{\alpha}(t,x) =   \E \edg{\int_t^T f(s, X^\alpha_s, \alpha_s) ds + F(X^\alpha_T) \, \Big| \,
	X^\alpha_t = x} .
\]
The following result shows how it can be learned from $G$.
\begin{proposition} 	\label{Prop2}
	Let $X^\alpha$ be the solution of the jump-diffusion equation \eqref{sde} corresponding to a Markov control $\alpha(t, X^\alpha_{t-})$ and  $V^\alpha \in C^{1,2}([0,T] \times D)$ the associated reward functional.  If $\E \, G^2 (t,X^\alpha_{t-}, Z_1, V^{\alpha}, \alpha(t,X^{\alpha}_{t-})) < \infty$
	for all $t \in [0,T)$, then
 \begin{equation}
		V^\alpha(t,X^\alpha_{t-}) 
		= \argmin_{\substack{g \colon [0,T] \times D \to \R\\g \mbox{ \scriptsize{Borel measurable}}}}  \mathbb{E} \edg{  \brak{g(t,X^\alpha_{t-}) - G(t,X^\alpha_{t-}, 
			 Z_1, V^{\alpha}, \alpha(t,X^\alpha_{t-}))  }^2 }. \label{lsmc2}
\end{equation}
\end{proposition}
\vspace{-2mm}
Our second method, GPI-PINN 2, leverages Propositions \ref{Prop1} and \ref{Prop2} 
to bypass the computation of the gradients $\nabla V_{\theta}(t,x)$, Hessians
$\nabla^2_x V_{\theta}(t,x)$ and jump expectations $\mathbb{E}^{\mathcal{Z}}[V_\theta(t,x+\gamma(t,x,Z_1,a))]$,
enabling faster numerical convergence for high-dimensional stochastic control problems 
with jumps. It approximates $g$ in \eqref{lsmc2} with a NN 
$V_\theta \in C^{1,2}([0,T] \times D)$ by minimizing the mean square error %$\mathbb{E}^{\mathbb{P}}\big[\big(V_\theta(t,X^\alpha_t) - \mathcal{G}(t,X^\alpha_t, Z_1, \alpha(t,X^\alpha_t)) \big)^2\big]$  
\eqref{lsmc2} with respect to the weights $\theta$, thereby bypassing the evaluation of the expectation $\mathbb{E}^{\mathcal{Z}}$ in the extended Hamiltonian $\cal{H}$. Finally, \eqref{lsmc2} suggests the following recursive scheme\footnote{Note that by \eqref{testest}, the update rule \eqref{notyet} can be seen as a continuous-time policy evaluation algorithm $V_{\theta^{(k+1)}} \hspace{-0.5mm}= \hspace{-0.5mm}T^\alpha V_{\theta^{(k)}} \hspace{-1.2mm}:= \hspace{-0.7mm}V_{\theta^{(k)}}\hspace{-0.3mm}+\hspace{-0.3mm} \mathcal{H}(\cdot,V_{\theta^{(k)}}, \alpha(\cdot))$, where $T^\alpha$ is a continuous-time Bellman operator associated with the Markov control $\alpha$ and fixed point $T^\alpha V^\alpha = V^\alpha$.} to evaluate the reward $V^\alpha$:
\begin{equation}
	\theta^{(k+1)}  = \argmin_{\theta}  \mathbb{E} \int_0^T \Big(  V_\theta(t, X^\alpha_{t-})  - G\hspace{-0.1mm}\big(t, X^\alpha_{t-} , Z_1,  V_{\theta^{(k)}}, \alpha(t, X^\alpha_{t-})\big) \Big)^2 dt . \label{notyet} 
\end{equation} 
%However, the recursive approach  \eqref{notyet} does not apply directly to implicit optimal control problems since the optimal control $\alpha^*$ does not admit an explicit solution that can be plugged back into \eqref{Ht}. 
%we now aim at developing a deep learning algorithm able to approximate via two separate NNs $\alpha_\phi$ and $V_\theta$ both the $m$-dimensional optimal control  and the value function in our CT stochastic optimal jump control problem \eqref{opt_cont} with finite horizon and continuous action space $A$. This algorithm will be specifically designed to perform well in a high-dimensional setting even when the optimal control does not admit an explicit representation, and will provide a global approximation of the optimal control and of the value function available across all time and space. This will be achieved by combining PINNs  together with an Actor-Critic structure,  built upon the two NNs $V_\theta$, $\alpha_\phi$ and based on GPI.   We first introduce the GPI-PINN Algorithm \ref{Algo 2} in Section \ref{sect3.2.1}, using PINNs on  the HJB residuals \eqref{HJB2} in an actor-critic fashion. This GPI-PINN  \ref{Algo 2} is then refined in Section \ref{section3.2.2} with the GPI-PINN \ref{Algo 3}, which leverages  Proposition \ref{Prop2}  to bypass the evaluation of the expectation $\mathbb{E}^{\mathcal{Z}}$ in $\mathcal{H}$, enabling faster numerical convergence in the presence of jumps.   \vspace{-2mm}
\section{Methodology} \vspace{-2mm}
This section presents a detailed description of our two algorithms, GPI-PINN 1 and GPI-PINN 2. 
To simplify notations, we denote when applied to the NNs $V_\theta$ and $\alpha_\phi$,
\begin{equation*}
	\mathcal{H}(t,x, \theta, \phi) := \mathcal{H}\big(t,x, V_\theta, \alpha_\phi(t,x)\big) \, , \quad G(t,x,z,\theta, \phi) := G(t,x,z,V_\theta, \alpha_\phi(t,x))
\end{equation*}
and introduce from \eqref{Ht} the extended expectation-free Hamiltonian 
\begin{equation}
	\mathcal{G}(t,x,z,\theta,\phi) := G(t,x,z,V_\theta, \alpha_\phi(t,x))- V_\theta(t,x) \, .  \label{key2}
\end{equation}
Notice that $\mathcal{G}$ is the counterpart of the extended Hamiltonian $\mathcal{H}$ when we do not consider the jump expectation $\mathbb{E}^{\mathcal{Z}}$ but only evaluate it at a single jump-size point $z \in E$.  
