\subsection{Linear-Quadratic Regulator with  jumps of controlled intensity}
\label{LQRex}
\vspace{-0.4mm}
We first discuss the linear-quadratic regulator (LQR) problem  
 \begin{equation}
\textstyle	\inf_{\alpha} \mathbb{E}\left[ \int_0^T c_1\, \|\alpha_s\|^2_2 \, ds + c_2 \|X^\alpha_T \|^2_2  \right] , \label{vv} \vspace{-0.1mm}
\end{equation}
where the infimum is over $d$-dimensional predictable  \vspace{-0.1mm}processes $(\alpha_t)_{0 \leq t \leq T}$ and $X^\alpha$ is a $d$-dimensional process with controlled dynamics  \vspace{-1mm}
 \begin{equation*}
\textstyle dX^\alpha_t = \alpha_t \, dt + \Sigma \, dW_t + d \sum_{j=1}^{M^\alpha_t} Z_j \,  , \ \  X_0 = x \in \mathbb{R}^d \, ,  
\end{equation*}
for a $d\times d$ matrix $\Sigma$, a $d$-dimensional Brownian motion $W$,  an independent Poisson process $M^\alpha$ with intensity $\lambda^\alpha_t =\Lambda_1 + \Lambda_2 \|\alpha_t\|^2_2\,$, $\Lambda_1, \Lambda_2 \geq 0$, and i.i.d.\ mean-zero square integrable $d$-dimensional random variables $Z_j$ with $\mathbb{E}[\|Z_j\|^2_2] = \zeta$, $\, j=1,2,\dots$ %This problem is of the form \eqref{pr} if instead of the infimum, one considers the supremum of the negative of the expectation in \eqref{vv}.  We treat in this section the case $\Lambda_2 =0$ while the results for $\Lambda_1 =0$ are discussed in Appendix \ref{lqr2}.
This problem admits a closed-form solution (see Appendix \ref{lqr2}), which provides a benchmark for our two numerical algorithms. We report their mean absolute errors with respect to the true solutions  $V$ and $\alpha^*$,  defined as ${\rm MAE}_V = \frac{1}{M} \sum_{i=1}^{M} | V_{\theta^{(k_{*})}}\hspace{-0.37mm}(t_i, x_i) - V(t_i, x_i) | $ and ${\rm MAE}_\alpha = \frac{1}{M} \sum_{i=1}^{\mathcal{M}} \| \alpha_{\phi^{(k_{*})}}\hspace{-0.4mm}(t_i, x_i) - \alpha^*(t_i, x_i) \|_2$ on a test set of size $M$, uniformly sampled from $[0,1] \times [-2.5,2.5]^d$. This leads to the following Figure \ref{comp1} comparing the ${\rm MAE}_V$ and runtime between the GPI-PINN \ref{Algo 2} and \ref{Algo 3} in function of the number of epoch $k$ for a $10$-dimensional problem ($d=10$) with controlled intensity ($\Lambda_2 > 0$). \vspace{-2mm}
