\subsection{Optimal Consumption-Investment with jumps}
While the LQR problem serves as a simple and illustrative test case, we now solve a more challenging class of optimal control problems with stochastic diffusion coefficients, a stochastic jump intensity process and both non-linearities in the controls and in the reward functions. In this optimal consumption-investment problem, an agent seeks to optimize 
\[
\E \edg{\int_0^T e^{- \rho s} u(c_sY^\alpha_s) ds + e^{-\rho T}U(Y^\alpha_T)} \, ,
\]
for two utility functions $u,U \colon \R^+ \to \R$, over $m=(n+1)$-dimensional  predictable processes $\alpha_t = (\pi^1_t, \ldots, \pi^n_t, c_t)$, where $Y^\alpha_t$ is the wealth process with dynamics \vspace{-0.3mm}
 \begin{align*} 
	&\frac{d Y^\alpha_t}{Y^\alpha_{t-}} 
	= \brak{r_t + \sum_{i=1}^n \pi^i_t(\mu^i - r_t) - c_t} dt + \sum_{i=1}^n \pi^i_t \edg{ \sqrt{\sigma^i_t} \  \Sigma_{S,i}^\top \, dW_t + d \sum_{j=1}^{M^i_t} \brak{e^{Z^i_j} - 1}} ,
\end{align*} for square-root   processes $r_t, \sigma_t,\lambda_t$. The strategies should then be of the form $c(t,\sigma_t, \lambda_t, r_t, Y^\alpha_{t-})$ and 
$\pi^i(t,\sigma_t, \lambda_t, r_t, Y^\alpha_{t-})$, leading to a $d=(2n+2)$-dimensional control problem with HJB equation \eqref{HJBinv2}, see Appendix \ref{optc2} for more details. There is no analytical solution available anymore in this more complex consumption problem, but we show with Figure \ref{fig:main4} that GPI-PINN 2 converges numerically.\vspace{-2.5mm}
