\section{Conclusions,  limitations and future work} 
In this paper we have introduced two iterative deep learning algorithms for solving finite-horizon stochastic control problems with jumps. Both integrate PINNs with an actor-critic architecture and train two neural networks to approximate the value function and optimal control, providing global solutions over the entire space-time domain without requiring simulation nor discretization of the system dynamics. Our first algorithm, GPI-PINN 1, works well for high-dimensional problems without jumps but fails to be numerically feasible in the presence of jumps. The second algorithm, GPI-PINN 2, introduces an efficient approach based on Proposition \ref{Prop2} specifically designed for high-dimensional problems with jumps and controlled jump intensities. Both algorithms are model-based and, compared to model-free RL methods, are especially well-suited in cases where state variables follow a known and given stochastic integro-differential equation. We have demonstrated  the effectiveness, scalability and accuracy of GPI-PINN 1 and 2 in different numerical examples. \\[0.155cm]
