\subsection{Optimal Consumption-Investment Problem -- Constant Coefficients}
\label{optcv}
As a second more advanced numerical example, we begin by studying an optimal consumption-investment problem with jumps in a financial market model with a risk-free asset evolving according to
\begin{equation}
	d S^0_t = r S^0_t dt  \, , \label{r}
\end{equation}
for a constant interest rate $r \in \R$, and with $n$ stocks whose price follows for $i=1,\ldots,n$, 
\begin{equation}
	\frac{dS^i_t}{S^i_{t-}} = \mu^i dt + \Sigma^{\top}_idW_t 
	+ d \sum_{j=1}^{M^i_t} \brak{e^{Z^i_j} - 1}  , \label{S}
\end{equation}
for some constants $\mu^i \in \R$, $\Sigma_i \in \R^{n} $, correlated\footnote{Note that $\Sigma = (\Sigma_1, \ldots ,\Sigma_n)^\top\hspace{-0.8mm} \in \R^{n \times n}$ can be seen as the upper  Choleski decomposition of the correlation matrix $\Sigma \Sigma^\top$ \hspace{-0.5mm}of the Brownian motion $W$.} $n$-dimensional
Brownian motions $W$, independent Poisson processes $M^i$ with constant
intensities $\lambda^i \ge 0$ and i.i.d.\ normal random vectors $Z^i_1, Z^i_2, ... , Z^i_{M^i_t}\sim \mathcal{N}(\mu^i_Z, \sigma^i_Z)$ with $\mu^i_Z \in \mathbb{R}, \sigma^i_Z \in \mathbb{R}^+$. Suppose an investor starts 
with an initial endowment of $Y_0 > 0$, consumes at rate $c_t Y^\alpha_{t-}$ and invests 
in the $n$ stocks according to $\pi^i_t Y^\alpha_{t-}$, $i =1, \dots n$. If the risk-free asset is used to 
balance the transactions, the resulting wealth evolves according to 
\begin{equation} \label{dynconst}
	\frac{d Y^\alpha_t}{Y^\alpha_{t-}} 
	= \brak{r + \sum_{i=1}^n \pi^i_t(\mu^i - r) - c_t} dt 
	+ \sum_{i=1}^n \pi^i_t \edg{\Sigma^{\top}_idW_t + d \sum_{j=1}^{M^i_t} \brak{e^{Z^i_j} - 1}}.
\end{equation}
Let us assume she attempts to optimize 
\[
\E \edg{\int_0^T e^{- \rho s} u(c_sY^\alpha_s) ds + U(Y^\alpha_T)} \, ,
\]
for two utility functions $u,U \colon \R^+ \to \R$ and $\rho \in \mathbb{R}$. Since the driving noise 
in \eqref{dynconst} has stationary independent increments,
it is enough to consider strategies of the form 
$c_t = c(t, Y^\alpha_{t-})$ and $\pi^i_t = \pi^i(t, Y^\alpha_{t-})$ for functions $c, \pi^i \colon [0,T) \times \mathbb{R}^+ \to (0,1)$, $i = 1, \dots, n$. This $n+1$-dimensional control is then written $\alpha_t = (\pi^1_t, \ldots, \pi^n_t, c_t)^\top  \hspace{-1mm}\in  A := (0,1)^{n+1}$. 
Assuming constant relative risk aversion (CRRA) utility functions $u, U$ with $\gamma \in (0,1)$, the reward functional is then denoted
\begin{equation}	
	V^\alpha(t,y) = \mathbb{E}\left[\int_{t}^{T}e^{-\rho(s-t)}\frac{\left(c_{s}Y^\alpha_{s}\right)^{\gamma}}{\gamma}ds+\frac{\left(Y^\alpha_{T}\right)^{\gamma}}{\gamma} \, \Big| \,Y^\alpha_{t}= y \right] .  \label{optinvesst}
\end{equation}
Finally, writing $\mu = (\mu^1, \ldots, \mu^n)^\top$\hspace{-0.5mm}, $\mu_Z = (\mu_Z^1, \ldots, \mu_Z^n)^\top$\hspace{-0.5mm}, $\sigma_Z = (\sigma^1_Z, \ldots, \sigma^n_Z)^\top$\hspace{-0.5mm}, $\lambda = (\lambda^1, \ldots, \lambda^n)^\top$, $\pi = (\pi^1, \ldots, \pi^n)^\top\hspace{-0.5mm}$, the associated HJB equation for the value function $V(t,y) = \sup_{\alpha} V^{\alpha}(t,y)$ satisfies for all $(t,y) \in [0,T) \times \mathbb{R}^+$,
\begin{align}
	0 = \hspace{0.4mm} & \partial_t V(t,y) - \rho V(t,y) + \sup_{(c, \pi) \in A} \bigg\{ (r+(\mu-r)^{\top} \pi -c) \, y \, \partial_y V(t,y)  \label{HJBinv}
	\\
	&\hspace{6.6mm} +\frac{1}{2}  \pi^\top \hspace{-0.5mm}\Sigma \Sigma^\top \hspace{-0.5mm} \pi \, y^2 \, \partial^2_{yy}V(t,y) + \sum_{i=1}^{n} \lambda^i \mathbb{E}\left[V(t, y+y \, \pi^{i} (e^{Z^i_1} - 1)) - V(t,y) \right] +\frac{(cy)^\gamma}{\gamma}\bigg\} \, , \nonumber
\end{align}
with $V(T,y) = y^\gamma/ \gamma$. This stochastic control problem does not admit an analytical solution but we can instead characterize its solution in terms of PIDE, so as to assess the accuracy of the proposed GPI-PINN algorithms. Following \citet{oksendal2019stochastic}, we assume the following form $V(t,y)  = A(t) y^\gamma / \gamma$ for the value function. First order condition on the HJB equation \eqref{HJBinv} then implies that  optimal fractions of wealth is a vector of constants $\pi^*(t,y) = \pi^* :=(\pi^{1,*}, \ldots,\pi^{n,*})^\top $ satisfying
\begin{equation}
	(\mu-r)^\top + \Sigma \Sigma^\top \pi^*  (\gamma -1) + \sum_{i=1}^{n} \lambda^i \, \mathbb{E} \left[ \left(1+\pi^{i, *} (e^{Z^i_1} - 1)\right)^{\gamma-1} \left(e^{Z^i_1} - 1\right)  \right] = 0 \, ,
\end{equation}
and the optimal consumption rate $c^*$ is independent from the wealth $y$ and given by
\begin{equation}
	c^*(t,y) = A(t)^{1/(\gamma -1)} \, .
\end{equation}
Plugging back these optimal controls inside the HJB equation \eqref{HJBinv}, we find that the function $A(t)$ satisfies the  PIDE
\begin{align*}
	\frac{	\partial_t A(t)}{A(t)} = \rho \hspace{0.5mm}- & \gamma \left(r+(\mu-r)^{\top} \pi^* - A(t)^{1/(\gamma-1)}\right) - \frac{1}{2} \gamma (\gamma-1) \pi^{*\top}\Sigma \Sigma^\top \pi^* 
	\\
	&\hspace{25mm}- \sum_{i=1}^{n} \lambda^i \mathbb{E} \left[ \left(1+\pi^{i, *} (e^{Z^i_1} - 1)\right)^{\gamma} \hspace{-0.5mm}-1 \right] - A(t)^{1/(\gamma-1)} , \nonumber
\end{align*}
with $A(T) = 1$, which can be solved numerically using \textit{e.g.}\ Runge-Kutta iterative methods RK45. For the GPI-PINN algorithms, we sample both time-space points independently from uniform distributions $\mathcal{U}_{[0,T]}$ and $\mathcal{U}_{[0,y_b]}$, respectively.  The parameters of the optimal investment problem below are as follows: $T=1, y_b=150, r=0.02, \rho= 0.045, \gamma =0.3, \lambda  = 0.45\cdot  \mathbf{1}_n, \mu_Z  = 0.25  \cdot\mathbf{1}_n, \sigma_Z  = 0.2 \cdot \mathbf{1}_n, \mu = 0.032  \cdot\mathbf{1}_n, \Sigma\Sigma^\top= 0.2 \cdot \mathbf{1}_{n\times n}$ with $\text{diag}(\Sigma \Sigma^\top) = \mathbf{1}_n$. Note that, compared to the LQR problem in Section \ref{LQRex}, the proportionality factor $\xi$ is now set to $10$. Moreover, since $A = (0,1)^{n+1}$, $\sigma_{\alpha_{out}}$ is chosen to be the sigmoid activation function. Instead of  the DGM architecture, we here train a classical feedforward neural network with 4 hidden layers, each of 128 neurons. Finally, the ${\rm MAE}_V$ and ${\rm MAE}_\alpha$ metrics are again defined as in Section \ref{LQRex} on a test set of size $\mathcal{M}$, uniformly sampled from $[0,1] \times [0, y_b]$ with $V$ and $\alpha^*$ obtained from the RK45 method described above.
\\
\\
We again compare in Figure \ref{compk} the ${\rm MAE}_V$ and runtime between  GPI-PINN \ref{Algo 2} and \ref{Algo 3} in function of the number of epochs $k$ for  $n=10$ stocks in the consumption-investment problem \eqref{optinvesst} with and without jumps. We again see that the residual-based GPI-PINN \ref{Algo 2} tends to be more stable and accurate for larger epochs, despite having a higher runtime. When accounting for jumps in the dynamics  \eqref{dynconst}, GPI-PINN  \ref{Algo 2} becomes numerically very time-consuming, even for a 10-dimensional control problem. This issue is  even amplified compared with the LQR problem (see Figure \ref{comp1}), as the jump size now depends on the control $\pi$ in the SDE \eqref{dynconst}. In contrast,  GPI-PINN  \ref{Algo 3} again handles these jumps much more efficiently.
