\subsection{Optimal Consumption-Investment Problem -- Stochastic Coefficients}
\label{optc2}
We then study a more complex optimal consumption-investment problem in a realistic market of the form
\eqref{r}--\eqref{S} with a stochastic interest rate
\[
dr_t = a_r(b_t - r_t) dt + \nu_r \sqrt{r_t} \,  \Sigma_r^\top dW_{t},
\]
with stochastic (Heston) volatility models for the $n$ stock prices
\begin{align*}
	\frac{dS^i_t}{S^i_{t-}} &= \mu^i dt + \sqrt{\sigma^i_t} \,  \Sigma^\top_{S,i} \, dW_t 
	+ d \sum_{j=1}^{M^i_t} \brak{e^{Z^i_j} - 1} \, ,\label{S2}	
	\\
	d \sigma^i_t &= a^i_{\sigma} (b^i_{\sigma} - \sigma^i_t) + \nu^i_{\sigma} \sqrt{\sigma^i_t}   \,  \Sigma^\top_{\sigma,i} \, 
	dW_{t},
\end{align*}
and with stochastic jump intensities
\[
d \lambda^i_t = a^i_{\lambda} (b^i_{\lambda} - \lambda^i_t) + \nu^i_{\lambda} \sqrt{\lambda^i_t}  \,  \Sigma^\top_{\lambda,i} \, 
dW_{ t} \, .
\]
Note that $\Sigma = (\Sigma_{S,1}, \ldots,\Sigma_{S,n}, \Sigma_{\sigma,1}, \ldots, \Sigma_{\sigma,n}, \Sigma_{\lambda,1}, \ldots, \Sigma_{\lambda,n}, \Sigma_{r})^\top \in \mathbb{R}^{(3n +1) \times (3n +1)}$ is the (upper) Choleski decomposition of the correlation matrix $\Sigma \Sigma^\top$ associated with the $(3n+1)$-dimensional Brownian motions $W$. In this case, the wealth process can still be described as follows
\begin{equation*} \label{dynconst2}
	\frac{d Y^\alpha_t}{Y^\alpha_{t-}} 
	= \brak{r_t + \sum_{i=1}^n \pi^i_t(\mu^i - r_t) - c_t} dt 
	+ \sum_{i=1}^n \pi^i_t \edg{ \sqrt{\sigma^i_t} \  \Sigma_{S,i}^\top \, dW_t + d \sum_{j=1}^{M^i_t} \brak{e^{Z^i_j} - 1}} ,
\end{equation*}
and the strategies should be of the form $c(t,\sigma_t, \lambda_t, r_t, Y^\alpha_{t-})$ and 
$\pi^i(t,\sigma_t, \lambda_t, r_t, Y^\alpha_{t-})$, with $\alpha_t = (\pi^1_t, \ldots, \pi^n_t, c_t)^\top  \hspace{-1mm}\in  A := (0,1)^{n+1}$. Hence, denoting the $(2n +2)$-dimensional process $X^\alpha_t = (\sigma_t, \lambda_t, r_t, Y^\alpha_{t-})$, its dynamics are given by \vspace{-1mm}
\begin{equation*}
	dX^\alpha_t = \mu_X(t, X^\alpha_t) dt + \Sigma_X(t,X^\alpha_t) dW_t + \gamma_X(t,X^\alpha_t)   \, d \sum_{i=1}^{n} \sum_{j=1}^{M^i_t} \brak{e^{Z^i_j} - 1}  ,
\end{equation*}
where
{\small \begin{equation*}
		\mu_X(t, X^\alpha_t)=\left(\begin{array}{c}
			a_\sigma(b_\sigma - \sigma_t) \\
			a_\lambda(b_\lambda - \lambda_t)\\
			a_r(b_r - r_t) \\
			Y^\alpha_{t-}(r_t +(\mu - r_t)^\top \pi_t - c_t)
		\end{array}\right), \,	\Sigma_X(t, X^\alpha_t)=\left(\begin{array}{c}
			\nu_\sigma	 \,\sqrt{\sigma_t} \, \Sigma^\top_{\sigma} \\
			\nu_\lambda \,\sqrt{\lambda_t} \, \Sigma^\top_{\lambda}\\
			\nu_r \,\sqrt{r_t} \, \Sigma^\top_{r} \\
			Y^\alpha_{t-}\sum_{i=1}^n \pi^i_t  \sqrt{\sigma^i_t} \,  \Sigma_{S,i}^\top
		\end{array}\right), \,
\end{equation*}} and
{\small \begin{equation*}
			\gamma_X(t, X^\alpha_t)=\left(\begin{array}{c}
			\bm{0}_n \\
			\bm{0}_n \\
			0 \\
			Y^\alpha_{t-} \pi_t^\top \mathbf{1}_n  \
		\end{array}\right) ,
\end{equation*}}with $\mu_X(\cdot) \in \mathbb{R}^{2n +2}, \Sigma_X(\cdot) \in \mathbb{R}^{(2n +2)\times(3n+1)}_+$ and $\gamma_X(\cdot) \in \mathbb{R}^{2n +2}$. Therefore, the value function
\begin{equation*}
	V(t,x) = \sup_\alpha \mathbb{E}\left[\int_{t}^{T}e^{-\rho(s-t)}\frac{\left(c_{s}Y^\alpha_{s}\right)^{\gamma}}{\gamma}ds+\frac{\left(Y^\alpha_{T}\right)^{\gamma}}{\gamma} \, \Big| \, X^\alpha_t =x \right] ,
\end{equation*}
satisfies the following HJB equation for all $(t,x) \in [0,T) \times \mathbb{R}^n_+ \times \mathbb{R}^n_+ \times \mathbb{R} \times \mathbb{R}^+$, 
\begin{align}
	0 = \hspace{0.75mm}& \partial_t V(t,x) - \rho V(t,x) + \sup_{(c,\pi) \in A} \bigg\{ \mu_X^\top\hspace{-0.2mm}(t,x) \, \nabla_x V(t,x)  +\frac{1}{2} \text{Tr}\left[\Sigma_X(t,x) \Sigma_X^\top(t,x) \, \nabla^2_xV(t,x)  \right] \nonumber
	\\
	&\hspace{6.6mm}  + \sum_{i=1}^{n} \lambda^i \, \mathbb{E}\left[V(t, \sigma, \lambda, r, y+y \, \pi^{i} (e^{Z^i_1} - 1)) - V(t,x) \right] +\frac{(cy)^\gamma}{\gamma}\bigg\} \, , \label{HJBinv2}
\end{align}
with terminal condition $V(T, x) = y^\gamma/\gamma$. We consider a portfolio of $n=25$ stocks, resulting in a $52$-dimensional value function and a $26$-dimensional control process, with the same parameters as in Section \ref{optcv}. This version of the consumption-investment problem is significantly more complex than the one discussed in Section \ref{optcv}, with the value function's dimensionality increasing from 2 to 52. Consequently, the RK45 numerical method can no longer serve as a reference point to solve the associated HJB equation \eqref{HJBinv2}, making it impossible to compute the ${\rm MAE}_V$ and ${\rm MAE}_\alpha$ metrics. However, despite this lack of a reference for comparison, the GPI-PINN \ref{Algo 3} still produces results that appear reasonable. We indeed first observe in Figure \ref{fig:main4} (main manuscript) that the losses $\widehat{\mathscr{L}}^{(k)}_1$ \eqref{How3} and  $\widehat{\mathscr{L}}^{(k)}_2$ \eqref{How4} converge as the number of epoch $k$ increases. Note that the orange curve in the left plot represents the interior loss (first right-hand term) of  $\widehat{\mathscr{L}}^{(k)}_1$  and the blue curve the boundary loss (second right-hand term) of $\widehat{\mathscr{L}}^{(k)}_1$\hspace{-0.3mm}, see Eq.\ \eqref{How3}.
\\
\\
The following Figures \ref{comp14} and \ref{comp15} confirm that the results from the GPI-PINN \ref{Algo 3} for both the value function and the optimal control are reasonable, as they closely resemble those from Figures \ref{comp4} and \ref{comp5}. For the value functions of Figure \ref{comp14},  the standard deviations across 10 independent runs of the GPI-PINN \ref{Algo 3} remain very low, confirming the stability of the approximations. For the optimal consumption rate in Figure \ref{comp15} (left plot), the standard deviation across the 10 runs tends to be higher for values of time $t$ close to 0, although being still reasonable. Finally,  varying the first dimension of the intensity $\lambda^1$ mainly impacts the corresponding fraction of wealth $\pi^{1,*}_t$, while its effect on the other proportions and consumption rate is more moderate (since arising from the correlation matrix $\Sigma \Sigma^T$ of the Brownian motions $W$).
