\section{Bulk Fluid Properties}\label{app:bulk_prop}

Here, we characterize the dynamic and structural properties of the solvent molecules (\text{\textbf{z}} type beads) in the bulk. %, by quantifying their self-diffusion coefficient and the viscosity of the fluid. 

%In the absence of an interface, the motion of DPD beads follows Gaussian statistics. 
\paragraph{Self-diffusion coefficient.} The self-diffusivity of the fluid \(\Dbulk\) is determined in the absence of an interface, from the mean-squared displacement of the DPD beads, %  as follows:  
%\begin{equation}
%\Dbulk = \lim_{t \to \infty} \frac{1}{6t} \langle |\mathbf{r}(t) - \mathbf{r}(0)|^2 \rangle,
%\end{equation}  
%where \( \langle|\mathbf{r}(t) - \mathbf{r}(0)|^2\rangle \) is an average of the MSD over all beads, 
which is displayed in Fig.~\ref{fig:dpflow_msd0}(a) as a function of time.
After an initial ballistic regime, motion is diffusive (\(\text{MSD} \propto t\)) for $t \gtrsim 0.1 \tau_0$. The slope of the line in the diffusive regime represents the self-diffusivity of the fluid, which we can quantify to be $D_b = 0.092 \ \ell_0^2/\tau_0$.


