\subsection{Variance and Probability Distributions}
Beyond mean trajectories, the variance $\mathrm{Var}[n_P(t)]$ quantifies the spread in product counts. Deterministically, there's no variance at all for a given initial condition. Stochastically, we observe:

\[
\mathrm{Var}[n_P(t)] \;=\; \langle (n_P(t) - \langle n_P(t)\rangle)^2\rangle.
\]

For a closed system, product eventually saturates at $n_{P,\text{max}} = n_{S0}$ (assuming complete conversion). The variance typically increases up to a certain point and then decreases as all substrate is consumed. For open systems (continuous substrate inflow), a nonzero steady-state variance emerges. System-size expansion or moment-closure can predict approximate analytic forms for these variances. Below is a an example of this coming from the same data as the previous simulation results. 

