\section{Introduction}

    Economic complexity measures provide valuable insights into the productive capabilities of countries and the structure of international trade~\cite{hidalgo2021economic, bahrami2023economic}. Two of the most widely used measures, the Economic Complexity Index (ECI) and the Economic Fitness and Complexity (EFC), have been instrumental in quantifying the economic diversification of nations and the ubiquity of products~\cite{cristelli2013measuring}. The foundational works by Hidalgo and Hausmann~\cite{hidalgo2009building, hidalgo2007product} introduced ECI as a method to infer a country's economic capabilities by analyzing the structure of the country-product network. Subsequently, Tacchella et al.~\cite{tacchella2012new, tacchella2013economic} proposed an alternative approach with the Economic Fitness and Complexity method, introducing a non-linear iterative framework to better capture the interplay between country diversification and product competitiveness~\cite{cristelli2015heterogeneous}. Later refinements of EFC, such as the regularized Non-Homogeneous Economic Fitness and Complexity (NHEFC) method~\cite{servedio2018new}, aimed to ensure robust convergence and improve its applicability to empirical data.

    While these algorithms have demonstrated strong empirical relevance in understanding economic growth and trade networks, their mathematical foundations remain an active area of research. Previous studies~\cite{caldarelli2012network, mealy2019interpreting} have explored the connections between economic complexity indices and spectral methods in network science. For example, in~\cite{sciarra2020reconciling} the authors show how ECI and EFC can be integrated into a unified measure incorporating information from both approaches, highlighting their structural similarities despite differences in algorithmic form. In~\cite{piccardi2018complexity, costantini2022measuring}, the authors investigate the relation between economic complexity indices and standard notions of centrality, showing that these algorithms can provide valuable insight into the spectral properties of the underlying graph. More recently, ECI and EFC have also been shown to be formally equivalent to network centrality measures~\cite{servedio2025fitness}. Extending these algorithms to arbitrary network structures, beyond the canonical bipartite formulation, has significantly broadened their applicability and interpretability. Therefore, a deeper theoretical understanding of these approaches is crucial to expanding their potential applications, as already demonstrated in fields such as ecology~\cite{lin2018nestedness, calo2025measuring, mougi2022predator}.

    Building upon these developments, recent studies have proposed a connection between the EFC algorithm and optimal transport theory~\cite{mazzilli2024equivalence}, particularly in relation to the Sinkhorn-Knopp algorithm~\cite{sinkhorn1967concerning}, which is used to compute doubly stochastic matrices. In this context, economic complexity indices can be interpreted as solutions to optimization problems analogous to those found in optimal transport~\cite{villani2008optimal, cuturi2013sinkhorn}, where cost functions play a central role in defining the equilibrium distributions~\cite{servedio2024economic}. Notably, the iterative structure of the NHEFC algorithm resembles the scaling procedures used in transport problems, and its associated cost function exhibits a formal analogy with Kantorovich potentials~\cite{villani2009optimal}, which characterize the optimal allocation of resources over networks. More generally, the interpretation of economic complexity algorithms as optimization problems~\cite{hartmann2002optimization, arora2015optimization, wright2006numerical} provides a unified framework that clarifies their mathematical structure, reveals properties of their solutions, and facilitates connections with other domains.

    This study demonstrates that both ECI and NHEFC can be reformulated as optimization problems, each minimizing a distinct cost function. Specifically, we show that the ECI algorithm minimizes an energy function derived from the network's Laplacian matrix. In contrast, we prove that the cost function underlying NHEFC is strictly convex, thus providing a rigorous foundation for its optimization-based formulation. 
    We also establish convergence to the fixed point—corresponding to the minimum of the cost function—in the case of regular graphs, and prove local convergence for general undirected and unweighted graphs. 
    In addition, we propose a modification to the iterative algorithm that directly follows the gradient descent of the potential, significantly accelerating numerical convergence.

    The results presented in this paper enhance the interpretability of economic complexity algorithms and pave the way for methodological improvements and new applications in network-based data analysis. 
    The reformulation of these indices as cost-minimization problems contributes to a more rigorous theoretical understanding of economic complexity and opens new perspectives for their application beyond traditional bipartite economic networks.

