For random sampling, we randomly select a subset of users for training:
$U_{\text{random}} \sim \text{Uniform}(\mathcal{U})$,
where $\mathcal{U}$ is the full user set.
For DBSCAN-based sampling, we cluster user embeddings and perform denoised sampling by excluding noise points and selecting users proportionally from valid clusters.
%510%$U_{\text{dbscan}} \sim \text{ProportionalClusterSample}(\mathcal{U}, \text{DBSCAN})$.
The probability of selecting a user $u$ is proportional to the size of their cluster:
$p_{u,\text{DBSCAN}} \propto |\{v \in \mathcal{U} : k_v = k_u,\, k_u \ne -1\}|$,
where $k_u$ denotes the cluster label assigned to user $u$ by DBSCAN, and $k_u = -1$ indicates a noise point. 
%This approach ensures representative \zm{user} sampling while reducing the impact of noise. \inote{why it can reduce noise}
\zym{This clustering-based strategy helps reduce the influence of outliers by discarding users labelled as noise ($k_u = -1$), which are typically far from any dense user group in the embedding space.}
