\subsection{Symmetries of the open system} \label{sec: symmetries of the open system}

Open quantum systems permit two distinct types of symmetries, weak (classical) and strong (quantum), distinguished by whether or not left and right symmetry-charges are separately conserved by the Liouvillian \cite{buca_note_2012, fazio_many-body_2024}. In the case of strong symmetries, there exists a steady-state solution for the density matrix in each symmetry sector, whereas for a weak symmetry, the density matrix still generically approaches a unique steady-state. Here, the open system possesses weak symmetries only, and in the exact quantum treatment presented in Sec.~\ref{sec: quantum treatment} for finite system sizes, we find numerically that the density matrix approaches a unique steady-state. Although the continuous rotational symmetry of the spins has been broken, in the following section, we define two important $\mathbb{Z}_2$ symmetries.

The first $\mathbb{Z}_2$ symmetry arises due to parity conservation (in this case referring to even or odd) of the total excitation number, since the non-number-conserving counter-rotating terms in $H_\text{SC}$ can only create or annihilate excitations in pairs. This is the relevant symmetry in the superradiant transition and, therefore, we refer to it as the ``superradiant parity'' (SR parity) symmetry going forward. Formally, the SR parity symmetry is a weak symmetry with respect to the parity operator $\Pi = e^{i \pi \sum_m S_3^{(m)}}$ for all forms of the Liouvillian: The Liouvillian in Lindblad form for the full spin-cavity model Eq.~\ref{eq: spin and cavity adjoint GKSL}, the Liouvillian for the spin-only model described by the Redfield master equation Eq.~\ref{eq: spin only adjoint Redfield}, and the Liouvillian in Lindblad form for the spin-only model in the fast-cavity limit $\kappa, \omega_c \gg \epsilon$ Eq.~\ref{eq: spin only Redfield fast-cavity}.

The second $\mathbb{Z}_2$ symmetry provides a rule for interchanging species and gives rise to the nonreciprocal phase transition identified in Ref.~\cite{chiacchio_nonreciprocal_2023}. For this reason, we refer to it as the ``nonreciprocal parity-time'' (NR $\mathcal{PT}$) symmetry going forward. The formal definition of the NR $\mathcal{PT}$-symmetry, however, is more subtle. For ease of explanation and transparent application in later sections, we restrict ourselves to a two-species model $M = 1$ and $M^\star = 2$ with balanced species populations $N_{-1} = N_1$ (and $N_0 = 0$). 

First, we define the involutive bipartite parity operator $\mathbb{P}$ which swaps tensor product operators corresponding to the two species, i.e. $\mathbb{P} \left( A^{(-1)} B^{(1)} \right) \mathbb{P} = B^{(-1)} A^{(1)}$ for generic spin operators $A^{(-1)}$ and $B^{(1)}$ acting on the Hilbert spaces of species $m = -1$ and $m' = 1$, respectively. All forms of the Liouvillian (see above) at equal and opposite phases $\Delta \phi = \phi_1 - \phi_{-1} = 2 \phi_1 \leftrightarrow - \Delta \phi = - \left( \phi_1 - \phi_{-1} \right) = - 2 \phi_1$ are related by the bipartite parity transformation $\mathbb{P}$. As a consequence, all forms of the Liouvillian have a weak symmetry with respect to $\mathbb{P}$ for phases $\Delta \phi = \phi_1 - \phi_{-1} = 2 \phi_1 = k \pi$ with $k$ an integer. In particular, for $k$ odd, a gauge transformation brings Eq.~\ref{eq: spin only adjoint Redfield} into the form of the standard two-species Dicke model, but may still exhibit interesting behavior resulting from the conservation of two collective spins and mixing of total-spin manifolds \cite{mivehvar_conventional_2024}. Additionally, Eq.~\ref{eq: spin only adjoint Redfield} has a (modified) weak symmetry with respect to $\mathbb{P}$ for phases $\Delta \phi = \phi_1 - \phi_{-1} = 2 \phi_1 = \left( k + 1/2 \right) \pi$ with $k$ an integer since the factors $e^{- i \Delta \phi} = \pm i$ can be made equal after a gauge transformation of one of the two spin species, e.g. $e^{i \pi S_3^{(1)}} S_\pm^{(1)} e^{- i \pi S_3^{(1)}} = - S_\pm^{(1)}$. Tuning the phases $\phi_m$ can break and restore the weak symmetry with respect to $\mathbb{P}$ and, therefore, the phases $\phi_m$ can be thought of as a ``chirality breaking'' parameters \cite{fruchart_non-reciprocal_2021}.

Second, we provide a formal definition of time-reversal in open quantum systems. Inspired by the generalized $\mathcal{PT}$-symmetry discussed in Ref.~\cite{huber_emergence_2020}, we define two similar involutive anti-unitary time-reversal operators  $\mathbb{T}$ and $\mathbb{T}'$. The former performs Hermitian conjugation $\mathbb{T} A \mathbb{T} = A^\dagger$, while the latter performs complex conjugation $\mathbb{T}' A \mathbb{T}' = A^*$.

Lastly, the combined operators $\mathbb{P} \mathbb{T}$ and $\mathbb{P} \mathbb{T}'$ provide two different realizations of the NR $\mathcal{PT}$-symmetry. First, the operator $\mathbb{P} \mathbb{T}'$ realizes a weak symmetry of the Liouvillian in Lindblad form for the full spin-cavity model Eq.~\ref{eq: spin and cavity adjoint GKSL} and the Liouvillian in Lindblad form for the spin-only model in the fast-cavity limit $\kappa, \omega_c \gg \epsilon$ Eq.~\ref{eq: spin only Redfield fast-cavity}. The Liouvillian for the spin-only model described by the Redfield master equation Eq.~\ref{eq: spin only adjoint Redfield}, however, does not exhibit the NR $\mathcal{PT}$-symmetry with respect to $\mathbb{P} \mathbb{T}'$ since retaining the energy splitting $\epsilon$ means the system samples the bath at the slightly shifted frequencies. The lack of the NR $\mathcal{PT}$-symmetry with respect to $\mathbb{P} \mathbb{T}'$, therefore, can be interpreted as resulting from the lack of Lindblad form. Second, upon removing single-particle incoherent decay $\Gamma = 0$, the operator $\mathbb{P} \mathbb{T}$ realizes a weak symmetry of the Liouvillian in Lindblad form for the spin-only model in the fast-cavity limit $\kappa, \omega_c \gg \epsilon$ Eq.~\ref{eq: spin only Redfield fast-cavity}. In this case, the results of Ref.~\cite{nakanishi_continuous_2025} under a Holstein-Primakoff approximation guarantee that the mean-field equations of motion, discussed later, possess a nonlinear $\mathcal{PT}$-symmetry. Reintroducing single-particle incoherent decay $\Gamma > 0$, however, explicitly breaks this realization of the NR $\mathcal{PT}$-symmetry with respect to the operator $\mathbb{P} \mathbb{T}$ since there is no balancing single-particle incoherent pumping term; Eq.~\ref{eq: spin only Redfield fast-cavity} is left with only the weaker NR $\mathcal{PT}$-symmetry with respect to the operator $\mathbb{P} \mathbb{T}'$.

We note a final symmetry which will be important in Sec.~\ref{sec: quantum treatment}. Since the spins are identically prepared and, in principal, not individual addressable, the system also has a weak permutation symmetry which formally arises from an $SU \left( 4 \right)$-symmetry of the Liouvillian superoperator \cite{xu_simulating_2013, hartmann_generalized_2016}. The symmetry is strong if there are no single-particle dissipative processes.

