\section{Preliminary results}

\subsection{Preliminary calibration for timing}


The GRAND antenna, called ‌the‌ HorizonAntenna, ‌is‌ optimized for ‌detecting‌ very inclined incident signals.  %likely introduced by the different response ‌of the‌ GPS chip ‌on the‌ FEB. 
Direction reconstruction in GRAND-like experiments relies on the early-late effect across triggered DUs, requiring precise GPS timing. Beacon tests using an LPDA (mounted on a container or mobile pole) revealed non-negligible timing offsets. We focus on a selected test for comprehensive calibration, governed by equation \ref{equ.SNR}, where $Dis_{i}$ and $Dis_{\text{ref}}$ are distances to No. $i$ and the reference DU, respectively, $c$ and $n$ are speed of light and the refractive index of air at site.

\begin{equation}
%\mathrm{T_{offset,ns}}=\mathrm{\frac{Dis_{DU_i-2-source}-Dis_{DU_{reference}-2-source}}{c/n} - (T_{DU_i} -  T_{DU_{reference} })}
T_{\text{offset,ns}} = \frac{Dis_{i} - Dis_{\text{ref}}}{c/n} - (T_{i} - T_{\text{ref}})
\label{equ.SNR}
\end{equation}

By comparing measured with expected signal arrival times between the beacon emitter and DUs, we determine individual timing offsets $T_{\text{offset,ns}}$. Statistical analysis of multiple test events yields a distribution whose mean and standard deviation represent the systematic GPS timing offset and resolution, respectively. Applying these corrections improves reconstruction performance, as evidenced by small dispersion on ground plane.(Figs. \ref{fig:timing}). Observed timing drifts and glitches \cite{PierreAuger:2015aqe} remain unaddressed in current analysis. Future upgrades will implement a more robust on-site timing system.

%Given that we ‌know‌ the positions of ‌the‌ emitter ‌used in the‌ beacon test and all ‌DUs‌, the relative time differences between expected and measured values enable us ‌to‌ know the offset for each DU. The $T_{\text{offset,ns}}$ ‌can‌ generate a distribution by accumulating more beacon test events. The mean and standard deviation values of the distribution ‌represent‌ the relative time offset and resolution of GPS. ‌These‌ two values could be used ‌in‌ reconstruction for better performance, as shown in \mkred{both Fig. \ref{fig:timing} and} \ref{fig:timing2}. The case of ‌using‌ offsets has ‌a‌ smaller reduced chi-square value statistically. We also noticed ‌the‌ existence of drifts of timing and ‌glitches‌, as ‌also‌ reported by other similar ‌experiments\cite{PierreAuger:2015aqe}, which ‌are‌ not taken into consideration at ‌the‌ current stage. A more flexible and robust timing system, ‌such as the‌ beacon test, will be built on site in the near future.

%The principle for timing calibration follows the formula \ref{equ.SNR}. Given that we have known the positions of emitter for beacon test and all DUs', the relative time differences between expected and measured values enable us know the offset for each DU. The $\mathrm{T_{offset,ns}}$ could generate a distribution by accumulating more beacon test events. The mean and standard deviation values of the distribution account for the relative time offset and resolution of GPS. Two values could be used into reconstruction for better performance as shown in Fig. \ref{fig:timing2}. The case of employment of offsets has smaller reduced chi-square value statistically. We also noticed that existence of shift on timing over time and glitch as well reported by other similar experiment, which is not taken into consideration at current stage. More flexible and robust timing system like beacon test will be built once our array is stable in the near future.


