\subsection{The galaxy contribution at low band on GP300}

\mkred{The Galactic radio emission in the GRAND band exhibits time-varying background intensities modulated by the celestial rotation, maintaining consistent Local Sidereal Time (LST) periodicity. Comparative analysis between 60-80 MHz observational data and RF-chain simulations reveals strong modulation alignment (Fig. \ref{fig:galaxy}), with residual discrepancies attributable to gain variations in the FEB's LNA/VGA components.}

%The Galaxy generates radio emission in the GRAND band. The rising and setting of the Galaxy with respect to our site contribute to ‌varying‌ background magnitudes over time, ‌with‌ modulation ‌that remains‌ constant in local sidereal time (LST) across different days. Below we compare real data from 60 to 80 MHz with RF-chain simulations, showing clearly consistent modulations between observational and simulated data (Fig. \ref{fig:galaxy}.) The differences between all DUs and simulations might ‌come‌ from the gains of the RF-chain\cite{GRANDhardware:2025atu}, which ‌are‌ provided by the LNA and variable gain amplifier (VGA) on the FEB in our experiment.}

The potential rotation angle deviations for each DU from nominal values (N-S/E-W/vertical alignments) critically impact cosmic ray detection accuracy, as they affect both individual antenna radio pattern reconstruction and RF-chain matching. Deployment variations and hardware iterations introduce angular uncertainties requiring systematic calibration - particularly for gain-rotation cross-optimization in our experimental framework.
